WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.365 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:07.275 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:16.820 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:23.360 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER: So it's my pleasure to
introduce Andrew Gunstensen, who

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.280 align:middle line:84%
I had the honor and
pleasure to work

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.080 align:middle line:84%
with for a long while
at Morgan Stanley.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.600 align:middle line:90%
And Andrew is actually MIT alum.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:44.320 align:middle line:84%
He got his PhD in
Geophysics from MIT.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:52.400 align:middle line:84%
And after that, he worked in a
variety of roles in financial--

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.700 align:middle line:84%
senior quantitative roles
in financial industry.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:01.020 align:middle line:84%
And now Andrew is head of
quantitative strategies

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.660 align:middle line:90%
at Mizuho in New York.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.583 align:middle line:84%
So Andrew, welcome, and
please take it away.

00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:07.500 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: OK.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.160 align:middle line:90%
Thanks, everyone.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.535 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
have a quick talk

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:12.600 align:middle line:84%
today about linear models,
and rates, in particular,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.180 align:middle line:90%
which is area I work in.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.680 align:middle line:84%
So we'll just have
a quick overview.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.097 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk a little
bit about some basic markets,

00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:20.860 align:middle line:90%
interest rates-- what are they?

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.180 align:middle line:84%
What are some of the more liquid
rates products, yield curves,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.300 align:middle line:84%
a little bit about hedging--
how you hedge your positions?

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.420 align:middle line:84%
P&L attribution, and then
end with a little bit

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.283 align:middle line:90%
of e-trading and bonds.

00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:32.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to caveat,
this is going

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.060 align:middle line:84%
to be a very nonmathy
presentation probably

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.840 align:middle line:90%
for this class.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.260 align:middle line:84%
So that's probably
my personal bias

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.997 align:middle line:84%
is I don't find math necessarily
that illuminating in some

00:01:41.997 --> 00:01:43.580 align:middle line:84%
of these problems
versus what problems

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.180 align:middle line:90%
are you actually solving?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.120 align:middle line:84%
As I told Peter, we were talking
about how to build a house,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.062 align:middle line:84%
we wouldn't spend 80% of the
time talking about hammers.

00:01:51.062 --> 00:01:53.020 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about how
big you want your rooms

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.100 align:middle line:90%
and how the connectivity is.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.067 align:middle line:84%
I would highly encourage
you to ask questions.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.900 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to ask Peter,
if nobody else does,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.560 align:middle line:84%
if he can pipe in a
couple just because I like

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.820 align:middle line:90%
to keep the discussion going.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.840 align:middle line:84%
And I'm generally
happy to yack on and on

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.980 align:middle line:84%
about any particular topic
that anyone is interested in.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.780 align:middle line:90%
So please talk away.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.980 align:middle line:90%
So start with the basics.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.180 align:middle line:90%
What are linear products?

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.840 align:middle line:84%
They are things that
are not nonlinear.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.880 align:middle line:90%
Nothing too surprises there.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.600 align:middle line:84%
Some of the major
categories are things

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.580 align:middle line:84%
like bonds, high interest rate
swaps, interest rate futures,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.400 align:middle line:90%
CDs.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.740 align:middle line:84%
These are the more traditional
relatively simple products.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.100 align:middle line:84%
These are not CDO-squareds or
callable spread range accruals,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.120 align:middle line:84%
or any of the super exotic stuff
that has been around for a while

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.970 align:middle line:90%
and still continues to be done.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.380 align:middle line:84%
No, the modeling is in some
sense relatively simpler here.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.467 align:middle line:84%
These are not huge,
multi-factor, stochastic

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.800 align:middle line:84%
calculus models you have to
do and term structure models

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.300 align:middle line:90%
and things.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.400 align:middle line:90%
It's relatively straightforward.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.600 align:middle line:84%
So the question, then,
is why do we care?

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.340 align:middle line:84%
As quants, why is
this interesting?

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.160 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to hazard a guess
as to why this stuff is still

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:54.870 align:middle line:90%
relevant today?

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.190 align:middle line:90%


00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.470 align:middle line:90%
Somebody take a guess.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.670 align:middle line:90%
You look like you're thinking.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because it's still
related to important stuff

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.470 align:middle line:90%
like interest rates.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:08.850 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: It is.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:09.850 align:middle line:90%
It's an important thing.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:10.930 align:middle line:90%
It's the core underlying.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.237 align:middle line:90%
What are some other reasons?

00:03:12.237 --> 00:03:13.570 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you might care about this?

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.110 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's traded a lot.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.990 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: It's a very
big market and a very liquid

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:17.490 align:middle line:90%
market.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:18.230 align:middle line:90%
True.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.310 align:middle line:84%
A couple other ones are
it's also generally used

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.210 align:middle line:84%
for hedging of the
more complicated stuff,

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.730 align:middle line:84%
and it's also an input to all
the more complicated models,

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.730 align:middle line:84%
which generally, you start
from the simple stuff

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.670 align:middle line:90%
and you build your models up.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:30.690 align:middle line:90%
These are very large.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.888 align:middle line:84%
These are the largest
markets on the planet.

00:03:32.888 --> 00:03:35.430 align:middle line:84%
Just a couple of statistics--
in the last few years, equities

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.750 align:middle line:84%
is getting close
now, particularly

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.110 align:middle line:84%
with the run-up in global
equities last couple of years.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:45.030 align:middle line:84%
Bonds have something north
of $120 trillion outstanding.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.190 align:middle line:84%
Interest rate swaps are more
like $500-plus trillion dollars

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.790 align:middle line:90%
outstanding.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.550 align:middle line:84%
The main futures contract,
more than $10 trillion.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:55.090 align:middle line:90%
These are very large numbers.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.770 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to
have a huge profit

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.730 align:middle line:84%
margin in a market this
size to do very well.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.570 align:middle line:84%
In a market of this size, it's
very actively-- it trades a lot.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.450 align:middle line:84%
The daily volumes, Treasury's
approaching $1 trillion a day.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.890 align:middle line:84%
Interest rate
swaps-- comparable.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.370 align:middle line:84%
Again, futures, something like
four million contracts a day

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.410 align:middle line:84%
in the SOFR futures, which are
the main short-term interest

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.890 align:middle line:90%
rate contracts.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.610 align:middle line:84%
And then I think a lot of
the more complicated models

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.750 align:middle line:84%
start with the simple stuff and
then build up on top of that.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.170 align:middle line:90%


00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.850 align:middle line:84%
And again, I have
a tendency to talk

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.288 align:middle line:84%
faster and faster and
faster if nobody comes back.

00:04:33.288 --> 00:04:34.830 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
apologize in advance.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.840 align:middle line:84%
And if you find me doing that,
somebody just tell me to stop.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.610 align:middle line:90%


00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.730 align:middle line:84%
And again, I said--
they mentioned they're--

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.490 align:middle line:84%
basically, they're
complex and inputs

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.910 align:middle line:90%
to the more complicated model.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.850 align:middle line:84%
Previously, I think they
were thought quite simple.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.110 align:middle line:84%
I think the global
financial crisis in 2008

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.390 align:middle line:84%
brought a number of
factors to the fore

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.710 align:middle line:84%
that people hadn't
considered before.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.350 align:middle line:84%
In particular, around
things like funding.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.650 align:middle line:84%
And what was previously,
say, a very simple product--

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.870 align:middle line:84%
so I'll pay you
$1.00 in 10 years.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.710 align:middle line:84%
How to value that became
quite difficult and subject

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:11.250 align:middle line:84%
to a lot of thinking
and discussion.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.150 align:middle line:84%
And there's not a
unique answer to that,

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.100 align:middle line:90%
which is also interesting.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.850 align:middle line:84%
A couple of the other
things that came in.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:23.790 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of regulation
that came in after 2008.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.750 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, somebody
said the regulators

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.430 align:middle line:90%
wanted to make banking boring.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.430 align:middle line:84%
And it's not boring,
but they've changed

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.030 align:middle line:90%
where the interest is in it.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.230 align:middle line:84%
And the super complicated
exotic products

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.590 align:middle line:84%
that were really
very much in vogue

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:39.170 align:middle line:84%
in the early naughts
and mid-naughts, really,

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:43.430 align:middle line:84%
those things ended poorly
for a lot of people.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.890 align:middle line:84%
You think about the causes
of the financial crisis,

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.990 align:middle line:84%
at least on the market
side, a lot of it

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:52.330 align:middle line:84%
ultimately boiled down to
US residential real estate,

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.690 align:middle line:84%
which had a huge run-up
and then an enormous crash.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.410 align:middle line:84%
But off of that,
there had been issued

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:00.410 align:middle line:84%
a lot of very complicated
securitization products,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.810 align:middle line:84%
very highly levered
and multi-layered.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.490 align:middle line:84%
And things were very
opaque and hard to value.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.095 align:middle line:84%
This was not financial
modeling's finest hour.

00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:11.970 align:middle line:84%
So I think there was a
lot of regulation that

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.345 align:middle line:84%
came in on top of that in
terms of model risk management,

00:06:14.345 --> 00:06:18.790 align:middle line:84%
and that really of ended
that section of the business.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.015 align:middle line:84%
So people went back a
little bit to basics

00:06:21.015 --> 00:06:22.890 align:middle line:84%
and started thinking a
little more about some

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.850 align:middle line:84%
of the fundamentals,
which I think

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:28.218 align:middle line:84%
was a good thing because I think
things were kind of silly there.

00:06:28.218 --> 00:06:30.010 align:middle line:84%
And I remember talking
to one of my friends

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:31.260 align:middle line:90%
at a soccer game or something.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.235 align:middle line:90%
Our kids were playing.

00:06:32.235 --> 00:06:33.610 align:middle line:84%
And he was mentioning
they'd just

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.810 align:middle line:84%
done a mortgage
securitization issue, which

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.130 align:middle line:84%
I believe defaulted without
making a single payment.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:44.930 align:middle line:84%
So this stuff was just so poorly
supported and thought out.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.290 align:middle line:84%
But because it was so opaque, a
smiling, fast-talking sales guy

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.790 align:middle line:84%
could push a lot of this stuff
to areas that really probably

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.030 align:middle line:84%
should not have been
involved in this area.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.470 align:middle line:84%
And you find little
municipalities in Norway

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.470 align:middle line:84%
having to declare bankruptcy
because they bought

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.790 align:middle line:84%
a whole bunch of CDOs from Iowa
strip malls or something crazy

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:02.290 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.710 align:middle line:84%
And you just think,
how did that happen?

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.350 align:middle line:84%
So I think there was
quite a lot of regulation

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.070 align:middle line:84%
came in, really, to put
a little bit of fences

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.930 align:middle line:84%
around some of this stuff,
which is quite powerful,

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.690 align:middle line:84%
but it can be used for
good or bad in some sense.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.590 align:middle line:90%


00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.670 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk a little bit
about, what is an interest rate?

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.350 align:middle line:84%
An interest rate,
at its most basic,

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:29.870 align:middle line:84%
is a rate at which you can
either borrow or lend money

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.413 align:middle line:90%
on a principal amount.

00:07:31.413 --> 00:07:33.830 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, I'm going to
have my interest, I, payment

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.310 align:middle line:84%
will be N, which is
a notional typically,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.910 align:middle line:84%
times the rate, r,
accrued over whatever time

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.630 align:middle line:90%
I'm borrowing it for.

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:41.350 align:middle line:90%
So I have--

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm borrowing $1,000
at 5% for a year.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:49.330 align:middle line:84%
In a year, I'll have to pay
back $1,000 plus 5% times a year

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:53.930 align:middle line:84%
times $1,000 with
$50 of interest.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.090 align:middle line:90%
Relatively straightforward.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.050 align:middle line:84%
How do I value that
in terms of what I'm

00:07:58.050 --> 00:07:59.850 align:middle line:90%
willing to pay for that today?

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.098 align:middle line:84%
I'm not willing to pay
$1,000 for it because--

00:08:04.098 --> 00:08:05.310 align:middle line:90%
well, let me reverse that.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.970 align:middle line:84%
So if I get $1,000 in a year,
how much am I willing to pay

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:09.010 align:middle line:90%
for that today?

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:11.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, whatever I'm willing to
pay for it today, I can invest.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:15.170 align:middle line:84%
And if you work out arithmetic,
it works out to be 1 over 1

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:16.510 align:middle line:90%
plus the rate times the time.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:20.930 align:middle line:84%
So at a 5% rate, I'll pay
something like $950-odd dollars

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.050 align:middle line:90%
for that.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.730 align:middle line:90%
And then in a year, get $1,000.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:29.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, this depends a lot on the
discounting rate, for example.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:32.409 align:middle line:84%
So what rate am I
going to discount at?

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:33.809 align:middle line:90%
That's hard to observe.

00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:35.892 align:middle line:84%
Rates I can get for investing
are easy to observe.

00:08:35.892 --> 00:08:37.851 align:middle line:84%
You walk into a bank,
and you look at the signs

00:08:37.851 --> 00:08:38.650 align:middle line:90%
in the front door--

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.510 align:middle line:84%
buy our CDs, 4.5% rates
for a year, whatever.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.340 align:middle line:90%
How much to discount is harder.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.880 align:middle line:90%


00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.440 align:middle line:84%
Typically, in our
world, we're going

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.680 align:middle line:84%
to assume one of the
institutional rates, which

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.700 align:middle line:84%
is something like a SOFR
rate or an OAS rate,

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.600 align:middle line:84%
which we'll talk more about
in a little bit in the future.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:01.383 align:middle line:84%
But these are a
standard assumption.

00:09:01.383 --> 00:09:02.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, one of the
interesting things

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:04.520 align:middle line:84%
practically is you
find every person has

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.160 align:middle line:84%
a slightly different rate that
they should be discounting

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.520 align:middle line:84%
because it depends on where
they can borrow money,

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.920 align:middle line:84%
and that depends on
your credit worthiness.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.580 align:middle line:84%
So you may have a
good credit rating,

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:14.900 align:middle line:90%
and you can borrow money at 4%.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:15.702 align:middle line:90%
You-- no offense.

00:09:15.702 --> 00:09:16.660 align:middle line:90%
I'm not picking at you.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:17.900 align:middle line:84%
You may have a
terrible credit rating,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.760 align:middle line:84%
so you have to pay
8% to borrow money.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:23.080 align:middle line:84%
You may have a medium one,
so you're borrowing at 6%.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:26.840 align:middle line:84%
And it all depends on the
particular issues of the issuer.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:32.040 align:middle line:84%
So this makes it interesting
because, at this point,

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.720 align:middle line:84%
a contract for you guys to pay
me $1,000 bucks in a year has

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.080 align:middle line:84%
different prices
depending on who you are.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.747 align:middle line:84%
So there's no single price
here makes it interesting.

00:09:40.747 --> 00:09:43.080 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the banks will deal
with this because it's kind

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.260 align:middle line:84%
of a pain in the ass for our
trading decks to have to manage

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.240 align:middle line:84%
1,000 different
counterparty relationships,

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.528 align:middle line:90%
each with their own funding.

00:09:48.528 --> 00:09:50.820 align:middle line:84%
So usually, you'll centralize
that in a central funding

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.140 align:middle line:90%
desk of some sort.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:56.300 align:middle line:84%
They will then assume all
of the individual traders,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.235 align:middle line:84%
can fund at a
relatively generic rate.

00:09:58.235 --> 00:09:59.860 align:middle line:84%
And the funding desk
will deal with all

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:03.340 align:middle line:84%
these horrible little funding
bases and small collateral

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:04.880 align:middle line:90%
exposures and things like that.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:10.460 align:middle line:90%


00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.740 align:middle line:84%
Going back a little
bit in history,

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.980 align:middle line:84%
you may or may not
have heard of LIBOR.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:16.940 align:middle line:84%
Probably not, because
it now is gone.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.260 align:middle line:84%
But this was one of the first
really common user rate.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.668 align:middle line:84%
Stands for London
Interbank Offered Rate.

00:10:21.668 --> 00:10:23.460 align:middle line:84%
It was developed, I
believe, by a Greek guy

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:27.660 align:middle line:84%
in the '60s, as a method of
standardizing syndicated lending

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.460 align:middle line:90%
in London.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.500 align:middle line:84%
Most bank lending to corporates
is floating rate lending.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:35.840 align:middle line:84%
So you won't do a fixed
rate loan for 10 years.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.340 align:middle line:84%
You'll do a rate that resets
every month, every three months

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.420 align:middle line:90%
at the then current rate.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.920 align:middle line:84%
And the question
then was, what rate

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.600 align:middle line:84%
should we be using to set the
interest payments on these loans

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:44.462 align:middle line:90%
at?

00:10:44.462 --> 00:10:45.920 align:middle line:84%
So what he came up
with is a notion

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.480 align:middle line:84%
we're going to poll the banks
as to where they lend money

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:49.140 align:middle line:90%
short term.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.390 align:middle line:84%
And we'll take the average
of that, kick out the highs

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.880 align:middle line:84%
and lows, and then that'll be
a standard rate everyone can

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.600 align:middle line:90%
use as a reference index.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.460 align:middle line:90%
It was a very, very good idea.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.440 align:middle line:90%
Incredibly successful.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.080 align:middle line:84%
First interest rate swaps, which
are really synthetic versions

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.520 align:middle line:84%
of those, came out
in the early '80s.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.180 align:middle line:84%
First century swap, I
believe, was 1980 or '81,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.663 align:middle line:84%
which is a cross-currency
swap with IBM.

00:11:10.663 --> 00:11:13.080 align:middle line:84%
And then the first pure single
currency interest rate swap

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:16.200 align:middle line:90%
came out the next year.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:20.600 align:middle line:84%
These products had phenomenal
growth in the '90s and naughts.

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.880 align:middle line:84%
Currently, there's something
about 350 or 400 trillion

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.200 align:middle line:84%
of them outstanding
as of LIBOR cessation

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.760 align:middle line:84%
and still continue to grow
a little more than that.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.500 align:middle line:84%
So very, very popular
product, very liquid,

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.440 align:middle line:90%
very actively traded.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:38.643 align:middle line:90%
LIBOR is a rate, though--

00:11:38.643 --> 00:11:40.060 align:middle line:84%
again, another one
of the fallouts

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.260 align:middle line:84%
from the financial crisis
showed that it had some issues.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.280 align:middle line:90%
LIBOR was set by polling banks.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:46.980 align:middle line:84%
So you'd go ask the
funding department

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:48.063 align:middle line:90%
at each of the big banks--

00:11:48.063 --> 00:11:50.188 align:middle line:84%
I think there was 20-odd
people in the LIBOR pool--

00:11:50.188 --> 00:11:51.855 align:middle line:84%
where are you going
to lend and borrow--

00:11:51.855 --> 00:11:53.660 align:middle line:84%
where are you going
to borrow money from?

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:54.910 align:middle line:90%
They've come up with a number.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:55.860 align:middle line:90%
They'd quote it.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.102 align:middle line:84%
Note that it's not based
on actual transactions.

00:11:59.102 --> 00:12:00.560 align:middle line:84%
So they're just
making a number up.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:01.820 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to, in
theory, if they're all

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.903 align:middle line:84%
good players, hitting it
where they would actually

00:12:03.903 --> 00:12:04.600 align:middle line:90%
do the trades.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.460 align:middle line:84%
But this is not actually
observable trades.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:11.580 align:middle line:84%
And I think in the late
naughts, early teens,

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.140 align:middle line:84%
a group of traders of
less than honorable intent

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:18.380 align:middle line:84%
realized that this is, and
this was a manipulatable rate.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, you may have-- for example,
every day you're going to look

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.620 align:middle line:90%
at how much money--

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.140 align:middle line:90%
these rates set every day.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.650 align:middle line:84%
And based on your portfolio,
you'll have some impact on that.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.900 align:middle line:84%
Some of them, you can have
millions of dollars a basis

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:32.000 align:middle line:84%
point, a basis point
being 100th of a percent.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you can get
that rate up or down

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:35.700 align:middle line:84%
just a little bit
in your favor, it

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.140 align:middle line:84%
can end up making
you a lot of money.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:40.400 align:middle line:84%
So what they did was
they essentially all

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:41.960 align:middle line:84%
chatted with each
other, and they

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:43.800 align:middle line:84%
were chatting with whoever
the people at their banks

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.340 align:middle line:84%
were responsible for setting
these rates and convincing them,

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.340 align:middle line:84%
look, I need you to make
this a high rate today,

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.520 align:middle line:84%
or I need you to make
this a lower rate today.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.680 align:middle line:84%
And can move this
thing a little bit

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.000 align:middle line:84%
because it's not observable
because nothing trades.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.760 align:middle line:84%
And they were doing that for
a while to their advantage.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.160 align:middle line:90%
This was discovered.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.100 align:middle line:84%
A number of them were
in jail for some time.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.400 align:middle line:84%
There was very substantial
multibillion dollar fines

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:05.760 align:middle line:90%
to the banks doing this.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.582 align:middle line:84%
But I think the realization
came that this rate didn't

00:13:08.582 --> 00:13:09.290 align:middle line:90%
have any support.

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:11.840 align:middle line:90%


00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.440 align:middle line:90%
One of the things is LIBOR is--

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.800 align:middle line:84%
it's an unsecured
rate, if you will.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.760 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have anything backing
it like a repo or collateral

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:20.720 align:middle line:90%
rate.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.560 align:middle line:84%
And there's just not
a lot of transactions.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.052 align:middle line:84%
Post-2008 nobody wanted to
lend unsecured because you

00:13:26.052 --> 00:13:27.260 align:middle line:90%
had a number of bankruptcies.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.920 align:middle line:84%
People like Lehman's
going out, and don't

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.100 align:middle line:84%
want to lend to money
who's nothing backing it.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.780 align:middle line:84%
So people weren't
happy with that.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.840 align:middle line:84%
And some of the things and
some of the tenors and markets,

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:40.980 align:middle line:84%
I think, had three transactions
in a six-month period.

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:41.800 align:middle line:90%
That's not enough.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.550 align:middle line:84%
But you have to quote
this rate every day.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:46.700 align:middle line:84%
So there's just
nothing fixing it.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.820 align:middle line:90%
So that was a real issue.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.100 align:middle line:90%
So the Fed set up--

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.710 align:middle line:84%
or the Fed plus the
other regulators--

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.460 align:middle line:84%
I guess it came out
of London, originally,

00:13:54.460 --> 00:14:02.300 align:middle line:84%
was the big driver for this, the
FSA there, LB, London and LIBOR,

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:04.143 align:middle line:84%
really decided you
needed a new rate.

00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:06.060 align:middle line:84%
It was going to be
defensible backing up based

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:09.780 align:middle line:84%
on actual transactions and
a number of other criteria

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.420 align:middle line:90%
to make it hard to manipulate.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.700 align:middle line:84%
So would be a genuine
real observed rate and not

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:17.220 align:middle line:84%
something that was just
being made up by banks

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:18.450 align:middle line:90%
to favor their trading desks.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.020 align:middle line:90%


00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: See-- just a comment.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:23.540 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Yep.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.700 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This LIBOR rate
may sound a bit esoteric

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:27.400 align:middle line:90%
at this point.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:31.820 align:middle line:84%
But at the time, mortgages,
mortgage interest rates

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.890 align:middle line:84%
were often quoted as
LIBOR plus a spread.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:41.090 align:middle line:84%
And my experience
was during the time

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:46.170 align:middle line:84%
of the naughts and the teens,
looking at possibly refinancing

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:50.330 align:middle line:84%
my mortgage to a lower rate
because rates were coming down,

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:53.170 align:middle line:84%
and the LIBOR did
not move much at all

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:57.230 align:middle line:84%
during that period, which was
rather frustrating to watch.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.590 align:middle line:84%
But then when understood
why it was not moving.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:02.290 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Well, it was
partly that and partly the LIBOR

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.370 align:middle line:84%
to Treasury spread-- which is
the difference between LIBOR

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.750 align:middle line:84%
rates, which are the bank
rates, and the Treasury rates,

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.050 align:middle line:90%
which are government rates--

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:11.290 align:middle line:84%
blew out in that period,
around 2011, '12.

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:14.450 align:middle line:84%
The fine institution I was
at at that point wrote off

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.630 align:middle line:84%
$8.4 billion in structured
notes because of that,

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:19.610 align:middle line:84%
for example, because they were
hedging their structured notes

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:22.330 align:middle line:84%
with LIBOR products and assuming
the two would stay together,

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:23.948 align:middle line:90%
and they went, whoop.

00:15:23.948 --> 00:15:25.990 align:middle line:84%
Most of it came back in
the next couple of years,

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:27.615 align:middle line:84%
but it was quite
unpleasant for a while

00:15:27.615 --> 00:15:31.890 align:middle line:84%
there and made them want to
change how they were doing that.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:33.947 align:middle line:84%
When we went through
the LIBOR retirement,

00:15:33.947 --> 00:15:36.030 align:middle line:84%
when you look at how much
of what kind of products

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:38.270 align:middle line:84%
are out there, there's
all sorts of weird stuff.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:40.150 align:middle line:84%
Swaps are the biggest
piece, but then you

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:42.110 align:middle line:90%
have floating rate mortgages.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:45.550 align:middle line:84%
You have a fair amount of
corporate bonds out there.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.110 align:middle line:84%
You have some really bizarre,
slightly esoteric stuff

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:49.150 align:middle line:90%
that is like--

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.430 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how you redo
that, going to go knock

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:54.910 align:middle line:84%
on somebody's door and tell my
mother that her LIBOR plus 100

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:57.210 align:middle line:90%
mortgage is now a SOFR plus 150.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.350 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what she's
going to do with that other

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.790 align:middle line:84%
than be confused and
call me and ask for help.

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:07.510 align:middle line:84%
So really, the LIBOR had
to go because it was not

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:08.830 align:middle line:90%
a good rate anymore.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.870 align:middle line:84%
And this is also true of
the equivalent LIBORs in--

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:17.130 align:middle line:84%
at least, Japan, Canada, US,
Switzerland, Europe, and the UK,

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:20.030 align:middle line:84%
I think all have replaced
their rates with something

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:22.150 align:middle line:90%
a little more defensible.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.310 align:middle line:84%
In the US it's
called the SOFR rate,

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:26.110 align:middle line:84%
which is the Securitized
Overnight Funding

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:29.990 align:middle line:84%
Rate, which is essentially
the average of overnight repo

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:30.870 align:middle line:90%
trades.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.070 align:middle line:84%
So a repo rate is
if I have a bond,

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.910 align:middle line:84%
I can just buy it
outright if I want to.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.090 align:middle line:84%
But it's not usually
an efficient way to do.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:41.430 align:middle line:84%
I can borrow money
against the bond.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:44.070 align:middle line:84%
So if I buy 100
million US treasuries,

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.450 align:middle line:84%
I can then lend that to
somebody, post that to somebody

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:49.050 align:middle line:84%
and borrow, say,
$98 million of cash.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.970 align:middle line:90%
He'll hold my bond.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.310 align:middle line:84%
And then if we want, we
can swap it back in again.

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:55.010 align:middle line:84%
But the rate at which you're
going to pay in that borrowing

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.270 align:middle line:84%
is known as the repo rate,
or reverse repo rate,

00:16:57.270 --> 00:16:59.450 align:middle line:84%
depending on which
direction you're going.

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:01.130 align:middle line:90%
And that's a real rate.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:03.970 align:middle line:90%
Real money changes hands.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.450 align:middle line:84%
US has about $800
billion of that a day

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:08.593 align:middle line:90%
of the main repo facilities.

00:17:08.593 --> 00:17:11.010 align:middle line:84%
So that's something that there's
a lot of transaction data

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:11.510 align:middle line:90%
behind.

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:14.710 align:middle line:84%
You can look at it,
measure it, add it all up,

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.040 align:middle line:84%
average it and come
up with a rate.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.040 align:middle line:90%
So that's what they did.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:22.516 align:middle line:90%


00:17:22.516 --> 00:17:25.630 align:middle line:84%
SOFR does have some features
that are not so fabulous.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:27.508 align:middle line:90%
First of all, it's a daily rate.

00:17:27.508 --> 00:17:29.050 align:middle line:84%
Nobody wants to have
a loan where you

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:30.342 align:middle line:90%
have to pay interest every day.

00:17:30.342 --> 00:17:32.810 align:middle line:84%
It's just a pain in the ass
operationally and not good.

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:34.810 align:middle line:84%
You want to pay every
three months or six months

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:35.950 align:middle line:90%
or annually, for example.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:40.590 align:middle line:84%
That just makes it just easier
mechanically to deal with.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.170 align:middle line:84%
You can compound
it into term rates.

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:44.110 align:middle line:84%
So we'll talk about
that in a slide or two.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:46.570 align:middle line:84%
Another thing, if you do
compound it into term rates,

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:49.090 align:middle line:84%
so you do like the average
SOFR over a three-month period

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:50.185 align:middle line:90%
and then pay that rate.

00:17:50.185 --> 00:17:52.310 align:middle line:84%
You don't know the rate
until the end of the period

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:55.630 align:middle line:84%
because you haven't finished
doing all your daily features.

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:58.473 align:middle line:84%
That's a bit annoying for
people, because a lot of people

00:17:58.473 --> 00:18:00.390 align:middle line:84%
want to know the rate
upfront so they can just

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:03.895 align:middle line:84%
arrange this operationally,
set all their payment systems,

00:18:03.895 --> 00:18:05.270 align:middle line:84%
make sure the
payments get their,

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:07.730 align:middle line:84%
schedule that, have
enough time to sort out,

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:09.870 align:middle line:84%
make sure they're
doing the right rate.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:13.110 align:middle line:84%
And the other interesting thing
is SOFR is a secured rate.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:16.430 align:middle line:84%
The banks actually
prefer to lend unsecured.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.730 align:middle line:84%
So I think when a
bank makes a loan--

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.730 align:middle line:84%
so it'll do a floating
rate loan to a corporate.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:22.310 align:middle line:90%
They'll give them $100 million.

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.310 align:middle line:84%
Corporate will pay them
the floating rate, say

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:27.590 align:middle line:90%
LIBOR plus spread or whatever.

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you think
about what happens

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.450 align:middle line:90%
when bank credit goes bad.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.970 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a crisis,
typically the bank spreads

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.555 align:middle line:84%
will blow out, which
means LIBOR will blow out.

00:18:37.555 --> 00:18:38.930 align:middle line:84%
And if the majority
of the income

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:40.805 align:middle line:84%
is coming from LIBOR
products, because that's

00:18:40.805 --> 00:18:44.090 align:middle line:84%
what you've loaned it,
you're not making more money.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:47.350 align:middle line:84%
So you loan money at LIBOR
plus 50 LIBOR is now 100.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:49.210 align:middle line:84%
You got an extra 50
basis points of income

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:51.210 align:middle line:84%
because your credit's
deteriorating.

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:55.422 align:middle line:84%
So it's a right-way risk
kind of feature that is--

00:18:55.422 --> 00:18:58.270 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if it's worth
a whole lot of the bank.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.870 align:middle line:84%
But it's a nice feature to have,
that if bank credit goes bad,

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:02.610 align:middle line:90%
you end up making more income.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:05.570 align:middle line:90%
So people like that.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.330 align:middle line:84%
Said LIBOR has mostly
been phased out

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.370 align:middle line:90%
in the last few years.

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:13.230 align:middle line:84%
US ended LIBOR last
June, so June 2023.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:14.390 align:middle line:90%
They're one of the biggies.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:17.210 align:middle line:90%
Sterling went earlier.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:19.010 align:middle line:90%
Canada went two months ago.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:21.108 align:middle line:90%
Mexico is going into this year.

00:19:21.108 --> 00:19:22.650 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a
couple of features

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:25.690 align:middle line:84%
in yen that are also happening
I think early next year.

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:27.650 align:middle line:90%
This was an enormous migration.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:32.072 align:middle line:84%
And you should hope in
your careers you never

00:19:32.072 --> 00:19:34.030 align:middle line:84%
deal with this kind of
stuff because it is just

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.110 align:middle line:90%
painful and tedious.

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:35.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:39.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you mind explaining
the relationship between bank

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:42.970 align:middle line:90%
credit and the funding rate?

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.430 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN:
So I'm a bank.

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:46.350 align:middle line:84%
Majority of my corporate
lending portfolio

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.390 align:middle line:84%
is floating rate
loans, which are

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:51.310 align:middle line:84%
going to pay me an interest
of LIBOR plus a spread, fixed

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:52.430 align:middle line:90%
spread.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:53.407 align:middle line:90%
So it's going to float.

00:19:53.407 --> 00:19:54.990 align:middle line:84%
If LIBOR is higher,
I make more money.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.190 align:middle line:84%
If LIBOR is lower,
I make less money.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:00.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to fund myself as
something that's typically not--

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:03.330 align:middle line:90%
less floating the [? rat. ?]

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.807 align:middle line:90%
So credit goes bad.

00:20:04.807 --> 00:20:06.390 align:middle line:84%
So banks get into
trouble, or somebody

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.950 align:middle line:84%
perceives that there's a
systematic issue out there

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:12.230 align:middle line:84%
with banking, like 2008,
or a couple other episodes.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:14.350 align:middle line:84%
The LIBOR spreads
are going to increase

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:18.017 align:middle line:84%
because larger spreads, higher
rates, lower quality, generally.

00:20:18.017 --> 00:20:20.350 align:middle line:84%
So what that means is the
spreads are going to increase.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.150 align:middle line:84%
My interest I'm going
to earn on my loans

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:26.140 align:middle line:84%
has now gone up, which helps
me because I get more income.

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:26.920 align:middle line:90%
Make sense, or--

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:27.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:27.660 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, thanks.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:27.920 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Yeah.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:28.440 align:middle line:90%
No, please.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:31.760 align:middle line:84%
Again, please-- I'm talking
fast on a big range of topics,

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:33.100 align:middle line:90%
so please jump right in.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:35.860 align:middle line:84%
You're looking
confused or anything?

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:36.402 align:middle line:90%
No questions?

00:20:36.402 --> 00:20:36.902 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:20:36.902 --> 00:20:37.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:37.980 align:middle line:90%


00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.325 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:39.325 --> 00:20:40.700 align:middle line:84%
You hope in your
career you don't

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.950 align:middle line:84%
have to deal with this because
it's a pain in the ass.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:45.900 align:middle line:84%
It's just an enormous
mechanical process.

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.320 align:middle line:84%
There's been a few
of them around.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:49.660 align:middle line:84%
Euro conversion was another
one in the late '90s we did,

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:54.410 align:middle line:84%
where all of the European
currencies converted to euros.

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:56.660 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I remember sitting in
Canary Wharf with that one

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:59.035 align:middle line:84%
at 3:00 in the morning because
we'd screwed something up.

00:20:59.035 --> 00:21:00.727 align:middle line:90%
And it was like, oh, dear.

00:21:00.727 --> 00:21:01.560 align:middle line:90%
But it all happened.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:03.820 align:middle line:84%
And then Y2K is
another classic one.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:06.020 align:middle line:84%
There's been a bunch of
other ones like that.

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:07.760 align:middle line:90%
But it all happened, and fine.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:08.610 align:middle line:90%
So no more LIBOR.

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:11.640 align:middle line:90%


00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.673 align:middle line:84%
So as I mentioned,
SOFR is a daily rate.

00:21:13.673 --> 00:21:14.840 align:middle line:90%
You don't want to pay daily.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.020 align:middle line:90%
So you can compound it up.

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:20.460 align:middle line:84%
Two standard ways-- I'm going
to do daily compounding.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:22.960 align:middle line:84%
So each day I'll work
out my discount factor

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:25.840 align:middle line:84%
for that day, which is 1
plus the rate over 360.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.820 align:middle line:84%
And you just compound those up,
and then subtract 1 off the end.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:31.400 align:middle line:84%
And that'll convert it to
an equivalent term rate,

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.320 align:middle line:90%
and you can pay the term rate.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:34.820 align:middle line:84%
Or I can just do
a simple average.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:39.480 align:middle line:84%
So just average the SOFR,
or average the daily SOFRs.

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:41.520 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the little
d's are the number

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:42.660 align:middle line:90%
of days it applies for.

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So a rate setting in Friday
will apply for three days

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:46.400 align:middle line:90%
over the weekend.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.920 align:middle line:84%
And a rate setting on Monday
only applies for one day.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:50.840 align:middle line:90%
Just do that.

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:51.580 align:middle line:90%
Add it all up.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.600 align:middle line:90%
Pay that rate.

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.800 align:middle line:84%
The majority of products
in the dollar market

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:58.920 align:middle line:84%
go on the compounding SOFR
because it's a simpler rate

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:00.240 align:middle line:90%
to calibrate.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.940 align:middle line:84%
It also doesn't
have the averaging

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.600 align:middle line:84%
you need to make a slight
correction due to volatility,

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:06.660 align:middle line:90%
which is just annoying.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:10.020 align:middle line:84%
It's a few basis points running,
so most people won't care.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:12.145 align:middle line:84%
But if you're trading this
a lot as a market maker,

00:22:12.145 --> 00:22:13.645 align:middle line:84%
you want to get all
this stuff right

00:22:13.645 --> 00:22:16.220 align:middle line:84%
because the bid offers are a
couple tenths of a basis point.

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:18.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you have something that
pushes you outside of that,

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:19.865 align:middle line:90%
you're going to lose money.

00:22:19.865 --> 00:22:22.340 align:middle line:90%


00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:25.500 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of other
rates out there.

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:30.140 align:middle line:84%
Term SOFR is one the CME
created a couple of years ago.

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:31.758 align:middle line:90%
They actually fit something.

00:22:31.758 --> 00:22:32.800 align:middle line:90%
They have a little model.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:34.960 align:middle line:84%
They do a best fit to all the
futures prices, which are again,

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:36.523 align:middle line:84%
are very liquid,
well-traded stuff.

00:22:36.523 --> 00:22:38.940 align:middle line:84%
And they work out one month,
three months, six month rates

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:40.460 align:middle line:90%
and then publish those.

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:41.720 align:middle line:90%
It's a set in advance rate.

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:44.160 align:middle line:84%
So I set it now, and it applies
for the next three months.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:45.700 align:middle line:90%
So I'll fix a term SOFR now.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:48.900 align:middle line:84%
Pay in three months at
that rate plus whatever.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:52.220 align:middle line:84%
This one is quite common
in corporate loans.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:53.760 align:middle line:90%
And again, corporate lending--

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:58.400 align:middle line:84%
the banks, in general, have much
better systems than the users.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:22:59.560 align:middle line:90%
Not always the case.

00:22:59.560 --> 00:23:02.185 align:middle line:84%
But they've been at this for a
while, where if I'm a corporate,

00:23:02.185 --> 00:23:05.180 align:middle line:84%
I don't necessarily want to deal
with a lot of the complexities

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:06.840 align:middle line:90%
and nuances of this stuff.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.313 align:middle line:84%
I just want to have
something fairly simple.

00:23:09.313 --> 00:23:11.480 align:middle line:84%
Every three months, I'll
look I rate up on a screen.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:12.380 align:middle line:84%
I'll put that on
my loan, and I'll

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:13.547 align:middle line:90%
pay that three months later.

00:23:13.547 --> 00:23:14.340 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't
know if this is like--

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.200 align:middle line:84%
if I'm not understanding
the English this correctly.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:25.720 align:middle line:84%
But let's assume we go back
to when rates were highest

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:27.680 align:middle line:84%
from now, like when
rates were-- when there

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:31.440 align:middle line:84%
was more of a risk of
rates going above 6%, that

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:37.440 align:middle line:84%
would directly influence how
the duration of the futures

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:38.920 align:middle line:90%
contracts.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:40.028 align:middle line:90%
Is that-- am I--

00:23:40.028 --> 00:23:42.320 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: I'm not
sure I'm understanding the--

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.695 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The
cheapest to deliver

00:23:43.695 --> 00:23:48.200 align:middle line:84%
for the futures contract changes
because the price, like the 6%.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:49.200 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Sure.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:51.760 align:middle line:84%
So at 6%, your futures
contract will [INAUDIBLE] at

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:54.380 align:middle line:84%
94 because the 100 minus the
price is generally the rate.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:54.600 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:55.120 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:58.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So then in terms
of thinking about how--

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.440 align:middle line:84%
do you ever think about how
that affects the term SOFR.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:09.800 align:middle line:84%
Theoretically, that would change
the duration of the cheapest

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.040 align:middle line:84%
deliver, which means that
your term SOFR represents

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:13.150 align:middle line:90%
a different rate.

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:14.400 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Term SOFR--

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:16.070 align:middle line:90%
it is a slightly different rate.

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:22.300 align:middle line:90%


00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:26.740 align:middle line:84%
So let me doodle here for a
little bit, if you don't mind.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:29.380 align:middle line:90%
So that's time, and that's rate.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.700 align:middle line:84%
So what it is, the futures
will each set a block

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:33.040 align:middle line:90%
of, say, three-month futures.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:34.915 align:middle line:84%
We'll set an average
over a three-month rate.

00:24:34.915 --> 00:24:36.900 align:middle line:84%
So I might have a
rate that does that,

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:39.540 align:middle line:84%
and another future that
does that and that.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.500 align:middle line:84%
Might have a little stub
period in here, which

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:43.780 align:middle line:90%
can go back to that future.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:45.160 align:middle line:90%
So each future will be set.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:48.340 align:middle line:84%
And it's essentially the average
over the term of the future.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:51.420 align:middle line:84%
So what CME then did was
they did a fit of this model

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:53.102 align:middle line:90%
to the futures contracts.

00:24:53.102 --> 00:24:55.060 align:middle line:84%
And they then interpolate
the daily rates, say,

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:59.340 align:middle line:84%
for six months, which will
run from here to about here.

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:00.820 align:middle line:84%
So they look at
that, and they'll

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:03.420 align:middle line:84%
work out what the equivalent
six-month term rate is based

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:09.540 align:middle line:90%
on these rates to compute that.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:11.900 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:16.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I think I'm
not asking this question.

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:17.363 align:middle line:90%
I did mean 1%.

00:25:17.363 --> 00:25:18.280 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: OK.

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:22.160 align:middle line:90%


00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:22.660 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:24.340 align:middle line:90%
So CME came up with that.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:25.440 align:middle line:90%
The loan guys like it.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:27.960 align:middle line:84%
Again, end user
corporates don't want

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:29.883 align:middle line:84%
to deal with all the
mechanics of daily sets

00:25:29.883 --> 00:25:30.800 align:middle line:90%
and operational stuff.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:32.920 align:middle line:84%
It's just a pain in
the ass for them.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:36.000 align:middle line:84%
So they're happy to let
somebody else do that.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:38.960 align:middle line:84%
It's the usual set of events--
pay six months or three months

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:39.460 align:middle line:90%
from now.

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:39.960 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:42.120 align:middle line:90%
That makes everyone happier.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.280 align:middle line:84%
The Fed, however, doesn't
want this to become

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:46.102 align:middle line:90%
a de facto new LIBOR rate.

00:25:46.102 --> 00:25:48.560 align:middle line:84%
So they actually have quite
severe restrictions on how much

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:49.898 align:middle line:90%
the dealers can trade in it.

00:25:49.898 --> 00:25:51.440 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, dealers
are only allowed

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:53.648 align:middle line:84%
to trade it to hedge their
loan portfolio, which also

00:25:53.648 --> 00:25:55.440 align:middle line:84%
is an interesting
feature, which means it's

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:56.720 align:middle line:90%
an entirely one-way market.

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:58.503 align:middle line:90%
Because banks issue loans.

00:25:58.503 --> 00:25:59.420 align:middle line:90%
They have to hedge it.

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:00.880 align:middle line:90%
It's all in the same direction.

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:04.080 align:middle line:84%
So there's a very substantial
basis between regular vanilla

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:07.090 align:middle line:84%
SOFR swaps and the term SOFR
swaps, something around four

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:09.100 align:middle line:84%
or five basis
points, which, again,

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.720 align:middle line:84%
in a market context, where
that's 20 or 30 times a bid

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:16.014 align:middle line:84%
offer, it's way outside
the bid offer range.

00:26:16.014 --> 00:26:18.030 align:middle line:84%
There's another
older version of SOFR

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:21.350 align:middle line:84%
called FedFunds, which is really
an average of slightly different

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:24.830 align:middle line:84%
FedFunds drawing, similar to
SOFR, that's still in wide use.

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:27.550 align:middle line:84%
And there's a whole lot of other
crazy rates-- prime, DISCO,

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:31.310 align:middle line:84%
municipal or military
housing rates and all sorts

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:32.125 align:middle line:90%
of other stuff.

00:26:32.125 --> 00:26:34.750 align:middle line:84%
DISCO's my favorite rate-- not
for any reason other than I like

00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:35.910 align:middle line:90%
the name--

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:37.540 align:middle line:84%
which is a Fannie
Mae discount rate.

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:43.888 align:middle line:90%


00:26:43.888 --> 00:26:44.930 align:middle line:90%
So now discounting rates.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:45.950 align:middle line:84%
One of the questions
is, how do you

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:48.050 align:middle line:84%
work out what the discounting
rate is going to be?

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:49.425 align:middle line:84%
For example, what
rate should you

00:26:49.425 --> 00:26:53.070 align:middle line:84%
use to discount
a future payment?

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:55.870 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume we have a
simple case of a fixed payment--

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:58.030 align:middle line:90%
F for fixed, at a time, t.

00:26:58.030 --> 00:26:59.670 align:middle line:90%
We assume some discounting rate.

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:03.190 align:middle line:84%
And we'll value the payment
today, give a present value of P

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:06.670 align:middle line:90%
Now, we have to fund this rate.

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:08.270 align:middle line:84%
Generally, in this
world, you assume

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:11.053 align:middle line:84%
that you are not just spending
money out of your back pocket.

00:27:11.053 --> 00:27:12.970 align:middle line:84%
You're going to go borrow
money from somewhere

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:14.747 align:middle line:84%
and use it to
purchase this thing.

00:27:14.747 --> 00:27:17.330 align:middle line:84%
So we'll buy P, and we're going
to buy at whatever our funding

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:17.750 align:middle line:90%
rate is.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.230 align:middle line:84%
So whatever we can
borrow money at,

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.250 align:middle line:84%
that's what we
we'll do to fund it.

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:23.690 align:middle line:84%
And now just think a little
bit about from one day

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:27.290 align:middle line:84%
to the next day, what are
the cash flows involved here?

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.650 align:middle line:84%
You have two of
them, essentially.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.330 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have to pay
money to borrow in your--

00:27:32.330 --> 00:27:34.347 align:middle line:84%
you will have to pay
interest on your borrowing.

00:27:34.347 --> 00:27:36.430 align:middle line:84%
So whatever interest rate
you've done for one day,

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:37.650 align:middle line:90%
you're going to pay that.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:39.530 align:middle line:84%
And then your
underlying asset, which

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:41.270 align:middle line:84%
is the fixed cash
flow in the future,

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:42.730 align:middle line:84%
will increase in
value a little bit

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:44.938 align:middle line:84%
because you're getting one
day closer to its payment.

00:27:44.938 --> 00:27:47.650 align:middle line:90%


00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:48.942 align:middle line:90%
So the interest paid--

00:27:48.942 --> 00:27:50.650 align:middle line:84%
the interest is just
going to be whatever

00:27:50.650 --> 00:27:52.370 align:middle line:84%
the present value
of that is, be it

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:55.790 align:middle line:84%
t0 times the borrowing
rate over 360.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.770 align:middle line:84%
So that's how much you're
paying to fund this.

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:59.570 align:middle line:84%
And the increase
in value is going

00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:03.430 align:middle line:84%
to go up by yesterday's value
times 1 plus r over 360.

00:28:03.430 --> 00:28:07.370 align:middle line:84%
That's the compounded
rate accrual factor.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:12.717 align:middle line:84%
Or the change in that is, again,
the value times r over 360.

00:28:12.717 --> 00:28:14.550 align:middle line:84%
Notice that the first
and the third equation

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:17.790 align:middle line:84%
have very similar forms here,
and the only thing different

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.893 align:middle line:90%
are the two rates.

00:28:19.893 --> 00:28:21.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is actually
going to imply

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:24.817 align:middle line:84%
those rates have to be the same,
or there's an arbitrage here.

00:28:24.817 --> 00:28:27.150 align:middle line:84%
So that means that your
discounting rate that you should

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.710 align:middle line:84%
be using to discount
on future cash flows

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:31.550 align:middle line:84%
is whatever your
funding rate is.

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:33.050 align:middle line:84%
So whatever you can
borrow money at,

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:37.225 align:middle line:84%
that's the appropriate rate
to discount future flows up.

00:28:37.225 --> 00:28:39.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, if those rates are
different, there's a trade.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.230 align:middle line:84%
You can either borrow
cheaper than you can invest,

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:43.170 align:middle line:84%
or you can invest cheaper
than you can borrow.

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:44.370 align:middle line:90%
And you do one or the other.

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:45.370 align:middle line:90%
And that'll come back.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:49.030 align:middle line:90%


00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:50.722 align:middle line:90%
This is a very--

00:28:50.722 --> 00:28:53.630 align:middle line:84%
if you only pick one thing
away from the hour and a half

00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:56.050 align:middle line:84%
we're spending here, remember
this because this is--

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:58.010 align:middle line:84%
funding and discounting
are the same.

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:01.405 align:middle line:90%


00:29:01.405 --> 00:29:03.530 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk a little bit
about some of the products.

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:05.238 align:middle line:84%
Just going to talk
about the vanilla one.

00:29:05.238 --> 00:29:07.110 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
how do I compute

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:10.530 align:middle line:84%
what I think forward
rates should be

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:12.592 align:middle line:84%
and what projections
of those should be?

00:29:12.592 --> 00:29:14.050 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going
to do essentially

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:17.570 align:middle line:90%
is build a curve to do that.

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:19.070 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start
with by thinking,

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:21.550 align:middle line:84%
what are the easily observable
instruments in the market?

00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:23.730 align:middle line:84%
What can I see out
there that I can think--

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.053 align:middle line:84%
whatever my pricing
model is going to be,

00:29:26.053 --> 00:29:27.970 align:middle line:84%
these are the things it
needs to hit because I

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:30.858 align:middle line:90%
can trade these things.

00:29:30.858 --> 00:29:32.650 align:middle line:84%
Some of the common ones
we're talking about

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:34.550 align:middle line:84%
are CDs, which are
Certificate of Deposits.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.850 align:middle line:84%
These are very short-term
bank lending, borrowing.

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:41.130 align:middle line:84%
You can walk into a bank and
buy a one-month CD at 4.5%

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:41.870 align:middle line:90%
if you want.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:43.778 align:middle line:84%
Maybe a three-month
one, a six-month one.

00:29:43.778 --> 00:29:45.070 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't go much beyond that.

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:46.690 align:middle line:90%
Maybe a year.

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:48.770 align:middle line:84%
Again, fairly
liquid, quite common.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:51.410 align:middle line:84%
Interest rate futures--
very liquid products.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:52.630 align:middle line:90%
You can do that.

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:55.307 align:middle line:84%
And finally,
interest rate swaps.

00:29:55.307 --> 00:29:56.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, so for each of
these, we're going

00:29:56.890 --> 00:29:58.850 align:middle line:84%
to work out what is
the price of this

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:01.810 align:middle line:84%
as a function of my
discounting curve.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:04.250 align:middle line:84%
And then given that,
the cash rates,

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.690 align:middle line:84%
I can invert that to find
what my discounting implied

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:08.870 align:middle line:84%
curve is that prices
all of these instruments

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:11.630 align:middle line:90%
back to market.

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:14.370 align:middle line:90%
So CDs-- nothing too hard here.

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:15.650 align:middle line:90%
I buy a CD.

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:19.550 align:middle line:84%
I get the amount I invested plus
the rate accrued over the time.

00:30:19.550 --> 00:30:22.110 align:middle line:84%
The value of that is that
forward interest amount

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:24.430 align:middle line:84%
plus the forward return
of my principal discounted

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:25.730 align:middle line:90%
back or inverted.

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:27.918 align:middle line:84%
I just get-- z is 1
over 1 plus r delta t.

00:30:27.918 --> 00:30:29.710 align:middle line:84%
That's again, that's
the thing you're going

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:32.750 align:middle line:90%
to see all over the place.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:34.510 align:middle line:90%
These rates are well-observable.

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:37.467 align:middle line:84%
They're a little bit tricky
because the swaps desk typically

00:30:37.467 --> 00:30:39.550 align:middle line:84%
can't necessarily trade
them because these CDs are

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:40.950 align:middle line:90%
funded products.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.390 align:middle line:90%
I have to actually buy them.

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:46.598 align:middle line:84%
If I want $100 million CD, I
need to somebody $100 million.

00:30:46.598 --> 00:30:47.390 align:middle line:90%
I have to get that.

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:48.515 align:middle line:90%
Swap desks don't like that.

00:30:48.515 --> 00:30:50.110 align:middle line:84%
They prefer
derivative instruments

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:52.550 align:middle line:84%
which are not unfunded
instruments because you just

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.350 align:middle line:90%
have to put less cash up front.

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:55.990 align:middle line:84%
And everybody likes
less cash up front

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:57.740 align:middle line:84%
because it means you
can do more, leverage

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:01.750 align:middle line:90%
yourself up more and so on.

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:04.110 align:middle line:84%
Second one is
interest rate futures.

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.250 align:middle line:90%
Again, very liquid contracts.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:09.650 align:middle line:84%
US ones trade on the
CME exchange in Chicago.

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:12.410 align:middle line:84%
Every country generally
has a relatively active

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:13.530 align:middle line:90%
short-term rate.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:16.690 align:middle line:90%
Canada trades in Montreal.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:19.070 align:middle line:84%
The UK contracts trade
on, I believe, the LME.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:22.290 align:middle line:84%
The Euro contracts trade on
the Eurex Japan contracts trade

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:24.020 align:middle line:84%
on Tokyo, I think,
Stock Exchange.

00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:26.730 align:middle line:90%


00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:28.910 align:middle line:84%
So two main
contractors-- there's

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:31.230 align:middle line:84%
one-month contracts and
the three-month contracts.

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:33.690 align:middle line:84%
They're slightly different,
which is a bit annoying.

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:35.910 align:middle line:84%
Three-month contract trades
on a compounded rate.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:40.050 align:middle line:84%
One-month contract trades
on an average rate.

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:43.330 align:middle line:84%
Three-month contract goes
from IMM date to IMM date.

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:46.065 align:middle line:84%
IMM is International
Monetary Market,

00:31:46.065 --> 00:31:47.690 align:middle line:84%
which was an old
market in Chicago that

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:51.130 align:middle line:84%
set the standards here,
which is the third Wednesday

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:54.010 align:middle line:84%
of every March, June,
September, December.

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:57.430 align:middle line:84%
It's just when the contracts
settle, and that's fine.

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.630 align:middle line:84%
The one-month contracts
trade on a calendar month.

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:01.570 align:middle line:84%
So today's contract
started in September 1.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:03.190 align:middle line:90%
We'll settle on October 1.

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:06.200 align:middle line:84%
I think I may be
off by a day there.

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.360 align:middle line:84%
The futures settle at
100 minus the rate.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:12.780 align:middle line:84%
So if I have an interest rate of
5% when I'm settling a future,

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:15.206 align:middle line:90%
that'll be a 95 price.

00:32:15.206 --> 00:32:18.400 align:middle line:84%
And again, people
just did it that way

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:20.320 align:middle line:84%
because they like to
know markets rallying,

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:21.420 align:middle line:90%
contracts go up.

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:23.920 align:middle line:84%
Markets selling off,
contracts go down.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.380 align:middle line:84%
For bonds, price and yield
move in opposite directions.

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:29.460 align:middle line:84%
So yields go up, prices
go down and so on.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:34.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's why they set
the 100 minus that.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:36.640 align:middle line:84%
There's a small convexity
adjustment here as well dealing

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:39.343 align:middle line:84%
with futures margining, which
I'm going to just wave my hands

00:32:39.343 --> 00:32:40.760 align:middle line:84%
and gloss over,
but it's something

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:42.520 align:middle line:90%
you have to be conscious of.

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:44.300 align:middle line:90%
Futures do daily margining.

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:46.442 align:middle line:84%
It's a standard part
of a futures contract.

00:32:46.442 --> 00:32:48.400 align:middle line:84%
So if I have an interest
rate futures contract,

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:51.600 align:middle line:84%
and I enter it into it,
and generally, you'll

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:53.960 align:middle line:84%
enter into a futures
contract at no upfront cost,

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:56.440 align:middle line:84%
aside from some initial
margin, perhaps.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:57.720 align:middle line:90%
Market moves up.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:00.280 align:middle line:84%
Futures contract
goes from 90 to 91.

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:02.220 align:middle line:84%
They'll give me
one back in margin.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:05.552 align:middle line:84%
If it goes from 90 to 89, I
have to pay one to remargin it.

00:33:05.552 --> 00:33:07.260 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of every
day, you settle up

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:09.220 align:middle line:84%
the net move on
that day, and they

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:13.620 align:middle line:84%
do this to minimize credit
exposure between them.

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.780 align:middle line:84%
But I think if I have an
interest rate contract, when

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:19.700 align:middle line:84%
the price of the contract goes
up, I'm getting money back.

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:23.540 align:middle line:84%
But the fact that the price went
up implies rates have gone down.

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:26.820 align:middle line:84%
So that means I can reinvest
my money now at a lower rate.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.417 align:middle line:84%
Or if the contract
goes down in value,

00:33:29.417 --> 00:33:31.500 align:middle line:84%
I have to fund that more
because I need to ante up

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:33.140 align:middle line:90%
more margin to the exchange.

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:35.620 align:middle line:84%
But I'm having to do that
at a higher interest rate.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:38.940 align:middle line:84%
So this correlation
between the flows of money

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.000 align:middle line:84%
and what it's going to
cost me to borrow or lend,

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:43.670 align:middle line:84%
that introduces a
small valuation impact.

00:33:43.670 --> 00:33:47.500 align:middle line:90%


00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk
about interest rate

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:51.180 align:middle line:84%
swaps, which are
probably the biggest

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:53.740 align:middle line:90%
single product on the planet.

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:56.340 align:middle line:84%
This is basically an
agreement, a custom agreement,

00:33:56.340 --> 00:34:00.560 align:middle line:84%
between two counterparties to
exchange two sets of cash flows.

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:04.560 align:middle line:84%
In the most simple case, one
of these is a fixed rate.

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:07.218 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to pay you
5% a year for 10 years.

00:34:07.218 --> 00:34:08.760 align:middle line:84%
And the other one
is a floating rate.

00:34:08.760 --> 00:34:12.518 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to pay you
in compounded SOFR.

00:34:12.518 --> 00:34:14.060 align:middle line:84%
I'll pay you annually
every 10 years.

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:17.933 align:middle line:84%
We'll do that for 10 years,
and then we'll call it a day.

00:34:17.933 --> 00:34:20.100 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many
variations of these structures.

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:21.700 align:middle line:84%
There's all sorts
of crazy stuff.

00:34:21.700 --> 00:34:23.480 align:middle line:84%
And imagination is the
limit here, as long

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:26.360 align:middle line:84%
as you convince somebody else
to do the other side of it.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:28.520 align:middle line:84%
They're a tremendously
useful instrument.

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:31.280 align:middle line:84%
They're great if I need to hedge
something or change my risk

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:34.239 align:middle line:84%
profile, it's a very
efficient way to do it.

00:34:34.239 --> 00:34:36.120 align:middle line:84%
These are all
derivative products,

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:38.020 align:middle line:84%
means they generally
don't use balance sheet.

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:40.063 align:middle line:84%
They don't take a lot
of upfront capital.

00:34:40.063 --> 00:34:40.980 align:middle line:90%
They're cheap to fund.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:45.159 align:middle line:84%
So they're quite popular
for those reasons.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.120 align:middle line:84%
And I said, they are the most
liquid interest rate product

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:51.000 align:middle line:90%
out there.

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:52.760 align:middle line:84%
They're one of these
things that there's

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:55.440 align:middle line:84%
a lot of mechanics
to get it right.

00:34:55.440 --> 00:34:57.743 align:middle line:90%
In particular, if you enter--

00:34:57.743 --> 00:34:59.660 align:middle line:84%
you work in this field,
you will get very good

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:03.512 align:middle line:84%
at data arithmetic,
which is a stupid thing.

00:35:03.512 --> 00:35:05.220 align:middle line:84%
I remember once I was
interviewing a guy,

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:07.738 align:middle line:84%
and he had just done a PhD in
numerical methods, I think.

00:35:07.738 --> 00:35:09.280 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
questions he asked me,

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:11.180 align:middle line:84%
which was a great question,
so keep this in mind

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:12.222 align:middle line:90%
when you're interviewing.

00:35:12.222 --> 00:35:15.520 align:middle line:84%
He said, if I come and work
for you, a year from now,

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:18.380 align:middle line:84%
what will I know then
that I don't know now?

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.480 align:middle line:84%
And I said will know how
to do data arithmetic.

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:23.078 align:middle line:84%
And he looked at me kind
of funny, went away.

00:35:23.078 --> 00:35:25.120 align:middle line:84%
And they actually gave
him a job, and he took it.

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:26.580 align:middle line:90%
And he's a fabulous guy.

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:28.788 align:middle line:84%
And he came back a year from
now, and said, you know,

00:35:28.788 --> 00:35:29.580 align:middle line:90%
you were right.

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:32.480 align:middle line:84%
I know how to tell between
my birthday and today

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:33.600 align:middle line:90%
how many days are there.

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:35.780 align:middle line:84%
It's not a unique answer
to that one, by the way,

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:36.947 align:middle line:90%
just to make it [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:36.947 --> 00:35:39.380 align:middle line:90%
It depends on your conventions.

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:41.860 align:middle line:84%
And I'm also going to guess
in interest rate swaps,

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.488 align:middle line:84%
at least 90% of all
problems are date-related.

00:35:44.488 --> 00:35:46.280 align:middle line:84%
That's the first thing
you want to look at.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:48.260 align:middle line:90%
Do I have the dates correct?

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:50.682 align:middle line:84%
Am I using the right
holiday calendars?

00:35:50.682 --> 00:35:52.140 align:middle line:84%
And holiday
calendars-- again, it's

00:35:52.140 --> 00:35:54.515 align:middle line:84%
another one of these things
that's occasionally annoying.

00:35:54.515 --> 00:35:55.700 align:middle line:90%
They change.

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.700 align:middle line:84%
We had Juneteenth added a
couple years ago, for example.

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:00.440 align:middle line:84%
We had the Queen's
funeral added.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.420 align:middle line:84%
Every time a president
or ex-president dies,

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:03.400 align:middle line:90%
there's a holiday.

00:36:03.400 --> 00:36:05.900 align:middle line:84%
And just these-- Canada had
Truth and Reconciliation

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:06.780 align:middle line:90%
they added recently.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:09.320 align:middle line:84%
So you have to
make sure you build

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:11.400 align:middle line:84%
your models to
account for the fact

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:13.440 align:middle line:84%
that the holidays
may be dynamic.

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So I just put on there a simple
standard dollar swap rollout.

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:18.040 align:middle line:84%
And there's a half
dozen different steps

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:20.415 align:middle line:84%
to actually get what are the
accrual periods and the cash

00:36:20.415 --> 00:36:22.640 align:middle line:84%
flows and the payment
dates and the reset dates.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:23.740 align:middle line:90%
You start with today.

00:36:23.740 --> 00:36:26.760 align:middle line:84%
You go to the trade date, if
it's a forward starting date.

00:36:26.760 --> 00:36:28.400 align:middle line:84%
From that, you
typically add two days.

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:29.608 align:middle line:90%
You go to the effective date.

00:36:29.608 --> 00:36:31.120 align:middle line:84%
You then add the
tenor of the swap

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:33.760 align:middle line:84%
to get the unadjusted
maturity date.

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:36.160 align:middle line:84%
You then roll that back in
increments of the payment

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:38.700 align:middle line:84%
frequency, get the
unadjusted roll dates,

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.200 align:middle line:84%
and then you adjust
everything based

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.908 align:middle line:84%
on a holiday calendar,
which is typically

00:36:41.908 --> 00:36:45.160 align:middle line:84%
going to be New York
banking or something.

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:47.998 align:middle line:84%
And again, you've got to be
really precise on the calendars.

00:36:47.998 --> 00:36:50.040 align:middle line:84%
Another one of the questions
I like to ask people

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:53.280 align:middle line:84%
is, how many distinct
dollar calendars

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:55.860 align:middle line:84%
do you think exist in terms
of swaps monitor, which

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:58.240 align:middle line:84%
is a standard source
for all calendar data?

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:00.540 align:middle line:84%
How many dollar calendars
do you think they have?

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:01.720 align:middle line:90%
Anyone want to take a guess?

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:05.340 align:middle line:90%


00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:07.037 align:middle line:84%
So I have New York
banking, for example.

00:37:07.037 --> 00:37:08.120 align:middle line:90%
I might have New York Fed.

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:11.610 align:middle line:90%
I might have-- somebody guess.

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:15.570 align:middle line:90%


00:37:15.570 --> 00:37:17.320 align:middle line:84%
There's no penalties
for getting it wrong.

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:21.680 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] OK, Peter,
what's your guess?

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:24.060 align:middle line:90%
You've been around for longer.

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:26.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Let's see.

00:37:26.740 --> 00:37:29.280 align:middle line:90%
I would guess maybe 150.

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.280 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: You're
in the right ballpark.

00:37:31.280 --> 00:37:33.820 align:middle line:84%
Last time I checked,
there was 294.

00:37:33.820 --> 00:37:35.877 align:middle line:84%
And every exchange has
got its own calendar,

00:37:35.877 --> 00:37:36.960 align:middle line:90%
and they're all different.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:39.327 align:middle line:84%
Every major business center
has its own calendar.

00:37:39.327 --> 00:37:41.160 align:middle line:84%
And it's just if you
get the wrong calendar,

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:43.077 align:middle line:84%
you're going to price
stuff on the wrong date,

00:37:43.077 --> 00:37:44.580 align:middle line:84%
and you're going
to misprice things.

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:46.060 align:middle line:84%
And then winner's
curse rule says

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:48.060 align:middle line:84%
if you price something
wrong, you only

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.940 align:middle line:90%
get the trade when it hurts you.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:56.060 align:middle line:84%
And again, dates are
just an enormous pain.

00:37:56.060 --> 00:37:58.310 align:middle line:84%
To value the swap, we're
going to do it in two pieces.

00:37:58.310 --> 00:38:00.490 align:middle line:90%
We'll start with the fixed side.

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:03.138 align:middle line:84%
So fixed side is
relatively straightforward.

00:38:03.138 --> 00:38:04.430 align:middle line:90%
We roll out all the cash flows.

00:38:04.430 --> 00:38:05.810 align:middle line:84%
We work out how
much we're paying

00:38:05.810 --> 00:38:07.290 align:middle line:90%
and what dates we're paying.

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:12.090 align:middle line:84%
We then, from our curve, get
the discount factors there.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:12.870 align:middle line:90%
Sum it all up.

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:15.390 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, I've just got
C, which is the coupon rate.

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:16.990 align:middle line:90%
Delta is the accrual fraction.

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:20.890 align:middle line:84%
So if it's an annual swap,
that'll be around one notional.

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:22.490 align:middle line:84%
And then times the
discount factor.

00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:23.110 align:middle line:90%
Sum it up.

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:25.440 align:middle line:84%
That's the present
value of the fixed side.

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:27.970 align:middle line:90%


00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Floating side's a little
harder but same idea.

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:33.290 align:middle line:84%
But instead of the
coupon rate, I've

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:35.188 align:middle line:84%
got the forward rates,
which are little f.

00:38:35.188 --> 00:38:37.230 align:middle line:84%
So I have to work out what
the forward rates are,

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:40.410 align:middle line:84%
but we'll get to that
in the next slide.

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:44.750 align:middle line:84%
Some mechanics about what are
the particular specifications.

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.230 align:middle line:90%
Is it set at the beginning?

00:38:46.230 --> 00:38:46.870 align:middle line:90%
At the end?

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:47.890 align:middle line:90%
Is it forward?

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:49.610 align:middle line:90%
Maybe a spread in there.

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:53.143 align:middle line:84%
What you end up with, there's
two different curves in here.

00:38:53.143 --> 00:38:55.310 align:middle line:84%
I need one curve to generate
the forward rates from,

00:38:55.310 --> 00:38:58.190 align:middle line:84%
and another curve to generate
the discounting factors from.

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:00.370 align:middle line:84%
In the vanilla swap, those
are both the SOFR curve.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:03.150 align:middle line:84%
So they're actually
only one curve

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:04.450 align:middle line:90%
So what do you want to do here?

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:07.335 align:middle line:90%


00:39:07.335 --> 00:39:08.710 align:middle line:84%
There's another
bit of magic here

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:12.190 align:middle line:84%
that I can actually
generate an implied floating

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:14.390 align:middle line:90%
rate from fixed flows only.

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:17.070 align:middle line:84%
So if I assume I can
borrow fixed rate bonds,

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:19.490 align:middle line:84%
I can borrow one at a
given-- at a first date,

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:21.730 align:middle line:84%
invest it, and pay it
back at a later date,

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:24.550 align:middle line:84%
invest it at the
unknown floating rate,

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:26.118 align:middle line:90%
and then pay it back at the end.

00:39:26.118 --> 00:39:27.910 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to assume
I'm going to invest

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:29.170 align:middle line:90%
at a rate that's a fair trade.

00:39:29.170 --> 00:39:31.030 align:middle line:90%
So PV of that is zero.

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:33.010 align:middle line:84%
So I end up here with--
and the initial term

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:36.070 align:middle line:84%
there is me borrowing
the money at time t0,

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:37.810 align:middle line:84%
me getting the interest
rate at time 1,

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:40.230 align:middle line:84%
and me getting the
principal back at time 1.

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:41.878 align:middle line:84%
So given all that
sums to zero, I

00:39:41.878 --> 00:39:43.670 align:middle line:84%
can work out what the
implied forward rates

00:39:43.670 --> 00:39:45.310 align:middle line:90%
from this discounting curve are.

00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:47.990 align:middle line:84%
So I use that, plug
it through the swaps,

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:52.050 align:middle line:90%
and now I've got a swap market.

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:55.150 align:middle line:84%
So for an implied swap markets,
generally when you do a swap

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:57.330 align:middle line:84%
it's usually done at
a 0 PV, or something

00:39:57.330 --> 00:39:59.333 align:middle line:90%
pretty close to a 0 PV.

00:39:59.333 --> 00:40:01.250 align:middle line:84%
So when I look at the
two legs and sum them up

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:05.110 align:middle line:84%
and make them zero, I've got the
fixed side plus the float side,

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:06.610 align:middle line:84%
both just the sum
of the fixed rates

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:08.530 align:middle line:90%
times the discounting factors.

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:10.250 align:middle line:84%
And then subtracting
the float side,

00:40:10.250 --> 00:40:11.770 align:middle line:90%
I'm just giving you factors.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:15.330 align:middle line:84%
Do some arithmetic, and I get
what the implied swap rate

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:18.330 align:middle line:90%
coupon is.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:20.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is useful because
swap rates are observed.

00:40:20.510 --> 00:40:22.052 align:middle line:84%
I go to my favorite
Bloomberg screen,

00:40:22.052 --> 00:40:23.750 align:middle line:84%
and I can see all
sorts of swap rates.

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:28.650 align:middle line:84%
So that's what I have to work
out my zero curve to fit those.

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:29.270 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:33.130 align:middle line:84%
I feel like I'm just motoring
through this here right now.

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:35.178 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:40:35.178 --> 00:40:37.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are these
slides [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:38.988 align:middle line:90%


00:40:38.988 --> 00:40:40.030 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Sorry.

00:40:40.030 --> 00:40:40.570 align:middle line:90%
What was the question?

00:40:40.570 --> 00:40:42.903 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to ask
if we could get the slides.

00:40:42.903 --> 00:40:44.070 align:middle line:90%
I don't know if they posted.

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:45.945 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: I
can certainly send them.

00:40:45.945 --> 00:40:47.370 align:middle line:90%
I don't think I have sent them.

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:49.932 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER: No, we don't have
them yet, but we'll get them.

00:40:49.932 --> 00:40:51.390 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN:
There is nothing

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:52.970 align:middle line:90%
too top secret in any of this.

00:40:52.970 --> 00:40:56.150 align:middle line:90%
So that's how you [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:56.150 --> 00:40:58.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So
Andrew, one comment.

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:04.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm not a fixed income expert,
but the zero present value

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:09.210 align:middle line:84%
at trade initiation is quite
an important feature, I think,

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:14.110 align:middle line:84%
where it encourages people
interested in either side

00:41:14.110 --> 00:41:19.838 align:middle line:84%
of the swap to be comfortable
with making the trade.

00:41:19.838 --> 00:41:22.130 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Yeah,
that's the so-called par rate,

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:24.090 align:middle line:84%
which is the rate that
prices are at par,

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:27.090 align:middle line:90%
a.k.a. for a swap that's zero.

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:29.090 align:middle line:84%
You could price it at
something other than that.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:32.110 align:middle line:84%
And again, bid offers in this
market are usually a tenth

00:41:32.110 --> 00:41:33.850 align:middle line:84%
of a basis point,
2/10 of a basis point.

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:35.710 align:middle line:90%
So it's around zero.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:37.230 align:middle line:84%
Some of the structured
transactions

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.090 align:middle line:84%
will have swaps that
are way off market.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:41.370 align:middle line:84%
And then that may get
accounted for elsewhere.

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:44.750 align:middle line:84%
Or that just may be where
you're taking your P&L out of.

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:47.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just another
comment that the--

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:52.130 align:middle line:84%
a long time ago, this was
like the late '80s, I was

00:41:52.130 --> 00:41:57.030 align:middle line:84%
at the Sloan School, and swaps
were becoming very popular.

00:41:57.030 --> 00:42:00.970 align:middle line:84%
And one of the reasons they
were becoming very popular

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:05.650 align:middle line:84%
was that certain corporations
could access certain fixed rates

00:42:05.650 --> 00:42:07.570 align:middle line:90%
at very low rates.

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:14.010 align:middle line:84%
And less creditworthy
companies could not have access

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:17.390 align:middle line:84%
to such good rates
for borrowing,

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:19.530 align:middle line:84%
but did have access
to floating rates.

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:24.410 align:middle line:84%
So these corporations
would pair up with a swap.

00:42:24.410 --> 00:42:27.632 align:middle line:84%
I don't know to what extent
the swap market now is.

00:42:27.632 --> 00:42:29.590 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: I
think it's well past that.

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:31.970 align:middle line:84%
But at the time, if
I'm a lower rated guy,

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:34.193 align:middle line:84%
and I can maybe
borrow short term,

00:42:34.193 --> 00:42:36.610 align:middle line:84%
but nobody's going to lend me
money long term because they

00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:38.237 align:middle line:84%
don't trust I'll be
around in 20 years,

00:42:38.237 --> 00:42:40.570 align:middle line:84%
but maybe they're comfortable
that I'll be here in three

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.570 align:middle line:84%
months, and another guy who
can then fund for 20 years

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:44.987 align:middle line:84%
because he's a
high credit rating,

00:42:44.987 --> 00:42:46.903 align:middle line:84%
we can both borrow in
our respective countries

00:42:46.903 --> 00:42:47.795 align:middle line:90%
and then do a swap.

00:42:47.795 --> 00:42:50.420 align:middle line:84%
So I now have fixed rate swaps,
and he has floating rate swaps.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:52.140 align:middle line:84%
And depending how they want
to manage their treasuries,

00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:53.807 align:middle line:84%
that may be a great
trade for all sides.

00:42:53.807 --> 00:42:55.120 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:57.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, swaps are very flexible
and let you move the risk around

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:03.040 align:middle line:84%
in quite a flexible way and
quite a customizable way.

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:07.800 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk now about the
core valuation model really

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.662 align:middle line:84%
in interest rate swaps,
which is the yield curve.

00:43:10.662 --> 00:43:12.620 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
parameterize the yield curve.

00:43:12.620 --> 00:43:14.440 align:middle line:84%
And the yield curve
is used to-- it's just

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:15.840 align:middle line:84%
to get a function
of the discounting

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:17.632 align:middle line:84%
as a function of time,
which then feeds all

00:43:17.632 --> 00:43:19.345 align:middle line:90%
of the other valuation models.

00:43:19.345 --> 00:43:20.720 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
parameterize it

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:24.650 align:middle line:84%
by a set of times and
zero coupon bond prices.

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:26.900 align:middle line:84%
You can parameterize it in
a number of different ways,

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:29.480 align:middle line:84%
but this is one
of the easy ones.

00:43:29.480 --> 00:43:34.380 align:middle line:84%
I also need to choose a method
to figure out what is my zero--

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:37.640 align:middle line:84%
the Zs, if I'm
between knot points.

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:39.220 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I have
annual knot points.

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:42.560 align:middle line:84%
So I'll do a 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
up to 10 years, for my times,

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:44.160 align:middle line:84%
and I'll have the
Zs at each of that.

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.940 align:middle line:84%
But suppose I need a 4 and
1/2 year discount factor?

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:48.260 align:middle line:90%
How do I do that?

00:43:48.260 --> 00:43:51.143 align:middle line:90%
Lots of possibilities here.

00:43:51.143 --> 00:43:53.060 align:middle line:84%
First of all, I'll talk
about the knot points.

00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:56.948 align:middle line:84%
How do you choose which times
to use to interpolate that?

00:43:56.948 --> 00:43:58.740 align:middle line:84%
Simplest one, which is
the most common one,

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:01.700 align:middle line:84%
is you just use the maturity
dates of all your instruments.

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:04.800 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to build it
with, say, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7,

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:05.600 align:middle line:90%
10-year swaps.

00:44:05.600 --> 00:44:06.660 align:middle line:90%
I have knot points--

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:09.020 align:middle line:90%
1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 10 years.

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:10.020 align:middle line:90%
Use that.

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:11.300 align:middle line:90%
That's the easy one.

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:13.500 align:middle line:84%
There's other possibilities,
like FOMC dates, which

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.100 align:middle line:90%
we'll talk about in a minute.

00:44:16.100 --> 00:44:19.260 align:middle line:84%
You also have to be a little
careful on picking your knot

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:21.580 align:middle line:84%
points to work well with
your interpolation method.

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:23.080 align:middle line:84%
Because some of
them, there's some--

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:25.220 align:middle line:84%
you can make bad
choices here, which

00:44:25.220 --> 00:44:28.533 align:middle line:90%
will give you unstable hedging.

00:44:28.533 --> 00:44:30.700 align:middle line:84%
We then are going to fit a
set of market observables

00:44:30.700 --> 00:44:32.780 align:middle line:90%
here to get the Zs.

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:36.620 align:middle line:84%
So given that we've chosen
an interpolation, our knots.

00:44:36.620 --> 00:44:39.940 align:middle line:84%
We have, hopefully, n knots
and n points we can solve.

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:42.400 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk a little
bit about CDF spline.

00:44:42.400 --> 00:44:44.502 align:middle line:84%
This is a constant
daily forward.

00:44:44.502 --> 00:44:45.960 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the
easy ones, which

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:47.668 align:middle line:84%
you assume that between
your knot points,

00:44:47.668 --> 00:44:49.440 align:middle line:84%
your daily forward
rates are constant.

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:52.280 align:middle line:84%
So you end up with a curve that
looks like a skyline or a stair

00:44:52.280 --> 00:44:53.460 align:middle line:90%
step or something.

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:56.780 align:middle line:84%
Just an example there, a
simple one, where in this case,

00:44:56.780 --> 00:44:58.940 align:middle line:84%
you can see what
my knot points are.

00:44:58.940 --> 00:45:03.220 align:middle line:84%
I think I have 6 months, a year,
2 years, 3 years, 5 years, 7,

00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:05.560 align:middle line:90%
10, and 20, I believe, and 30.

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:08.840 align:middle line:84%
And the forward rates are
just flat in between them.

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:10.080 align:middle line:90%
This is perfectly good.

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:13.000 align:middle line:90%
This is really fast.

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:15.120 align:middle line:84%
This is a local
interpolation method, which

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:16.800 align:middle line:90%
is one of the key features.

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:19.700 align:middle line:84%
And what I mean by that
is if I'm looking at--

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:22.960 align:middle line:84%
I want to find a discount
factor say, or there, it only

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:25.460 align:middle line:84%
depends on the value
there and the value there.

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:27.755 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend on anything
here or anything there.

00:45:27.755 --> 00:45:29.880 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to
interpolate between those two.

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:32.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why do you not
choose to not [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:32.080 --> 00:45:33.875 align:middle line:90%
based on minimizing error?

00:45:33.875 --> 00:45:35.000 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: On what?

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:36.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: On minimizing error.

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:39.100 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: How
are you going to do that?

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:41.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like
Chebyshev, I guess.

00:45:41.380 --> 00:45:42.820 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: You could.

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:45.060 align:middle line:84%
What you want to do-- the
thing-- on the simple case

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:46.820 align:middle line:84%
is you want to maintain a
connection with your knot

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:48.700 align:middle line:84%
point and your
calibration instruments.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:50.760 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, I'm
choosing the maturities.

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:52.260 align:middle line:90%
You can do lots of other stuff.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:54.010 align:middle line:84%
But you could-- for
example, let's suppose

00:45:54.010 --> 00:45:56.700 align:middle line:84%
you had this set of
instruments according

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:58.780 align:middle line:90%
to the knot points here.

00:45:58.780 --> 00:46:02.800 align:middle line:84%
If I choose, say, knot points
at 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30,

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.398 align:middle line:84%
I'm overspecified here, and
I'm underspecified there.

00:46:05.398 --> 00:46:06.940 align:middle line:84%
So you need to choose
it with regards

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:09.523 align:middle line:84%
to how you're going to
calibrate, particularly.

00:46:09.523 --> 00:46:10.940 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing
that comes out

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:12.900 align:middle line:84%
is when I work out in
the simplest cases what

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:14.780 align:middle line:84%
my hedges are, it's
in terms of my yield

00:46:14.780 --> 00:46:16.340 align:middle line:90%
curve input instruments.

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:18.780 align:middle line:84%
So I want to choose a good set
of liquid, observable stuff

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:21.940 align:middle line:84%
that I can actually transact
in to do the hedging.

00:46:21.940 --> 00:46:24.300 align:middle line:84%
There's a zillion ways to
do this, and that's still--

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:26.540 align:middle line:84%
30 years in, that's
still a very active area

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:27.840 align:middle line:90%
of research and discussion.

00:46:27.840 --> 00:46:30.600 align:middle line:90%
So lots of stuff.

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:34.760 align:middle line:84%
And some of the areas, like
some of the computer graphics,

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:37.920 align:middle line:84%
for example, has a lot of
advanced interpolation stuff,

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:38.980 align:middle line:90%
B-splines and beyond.

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:42.320 align:middle line:84%
And all of those are likely
entirely relevant here.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:44.000 align:middle line:90%
Just haven't looked at it.

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:49.180 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so this one gets you
your very characteristic shape.

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:54.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is probably the least
smooth, simplest, fast method.

00:46:54.320 --> 00:46:58.120 align:middle line:84%
So it's got popularities
for that reason.

00:46:58.120 --> 00:47:00.520 align:middle line:84%
On the other side, the
most smooth, little slower

00:47:00.520 --> 00:47:01.337 align:middle line:90%
is cubic splines.

00:47:01.337 --> 00:47:02.920 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, I'm
just going to fit

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:05.520 align:middle line:84%
a piecewise cubic
polynomial here.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.020 align:middle line:84%
So I'll work out what my
little parameters are.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:10.180 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm just
going to sum it up.

00:47:10.180 --> 00:47:12.480 align:middle line:84%
I have four terms,
cubic in each interval.

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:13.920 align:middle line:84%
I impose constraints
at the edges

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:17.480 align:middle line:84%
of the intervals on continuity
and smoothness and do that.

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:19.500 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is still a
reasonably fast method.

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:21.920 align:middle line:90%
Gives a very smooth curve.

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:23.500 align:middle line:90%
Now, this curve is not local.

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:24.840 align:middle line:90%
This is a global curve.

00:47:24.840 --> 00:47:27.620 align:middle line:84%
And if any of you go into
interest rate quant space,

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:30.280 align:middle line:84%
you need to make up little
laminated cards saying,

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:33.560 align:middle line:84%
I can have fast,
local hedges, or I

00:47:33.560 --> 00:47:35.400 align:middle line:90%
can have smooth, global hedges.

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:36.880 align:middle line:84%
And it can be
anywhere in between.

00:47:36.880 --> 00:47:40.020 align:middle line:84%
But I've had this argument
for 30 years with traders.

00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:42.060 align:middle line:84%
Traders always want-- if
I'm going to do a hedge

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:44.477 align:middle line:84%
of an eight-year instrument,
they want it to be only seven

00:47:44.477 --> 00:47:45.305 align:middle line:90%
and 10-year hedges.

00:47:45.305 --> 00:47:47.180 align:middle line:84%
They don't like it when
the hedges spill over

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:49.000 align:middle line:90%
to five years or 15 years.

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:53.100 align:middle line:84%
But smoothness is
a global property.

00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:57.780 align:middle line:84%
So if this is my curve, and I
have a point, some instrument

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:01.423 align:middle line:84%
in this area and
I'm pricing it, it's

00:48:01.423 --> 00:48:02.840 align:middle line:84%
affected, obviously,
by these two.

00:48:02.840 --> 00:48:05.000 align:middle line:84%
But if this point
here was down here,

00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:07.420 align:middle line:84%
I would have a different
shape through that area

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:10.040 align:middle line:84%
because I'm maintaining
smoothness, again global.

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:12.020 align:middle line:84%
So what the global
thing implies is

00:48:12.020 --> 00:48:14.780 align:middle line:84%
that I'm sensitive to
points much farther out

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:17.020 align:middle line:90%
from my maturity point.

00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:21.377 align:middle line:84%
And again, this is an argument
we've had for decades.

00:48:21.377 --> 00:48:23.460 align:middle line:84%
Some of the smooth methods
are better than others.

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:26.260 align:middle line:84%
Cubic spline is
probably the worst

00:48:26.260 --> 00:48:27.560 align:middle line:90%
because it is the most smooth.

00:48:27.560 --> 00:48:29.643 align:middle line:84%
I guess you could do a
quintic spline or something

00:48:29.643 --> 00:48:31.440 align:middle line:84%
if you really wanted
to be pathologic,

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:35.610 align:middle line:84%
but that would be a
weird thing to do.

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:36.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:38.170 align:middle line:90%
So then, how do I calibrate?

00:48:38.170 --> 00:48:39.610 align:middle line:90%
I can write my error function.

00:48:39.610 --> 00:48:41.610 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to price each
instrument as a function

00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:43.818 align:middle line:90%
of my proposed curve.

00:48:43.818 --> 00:48:46.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to from that take
the difference between that

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:48.850 align:middle line:84%
and what the actual
observed price is.

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:53.170 align:middle line:84%
So for-- I can then just-- if I
have n knots and n calibration

00:48:53.170 --> 00:48:55.050 align:middle line:84%
instrument, I just
solve that exactly.

00:48:55.050 --> 00:48:58.112 align:middle line:84%
Pick your favorite numerical
solver, and off you go.

00:48:58.112 --> 00:48:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Or, in some cases,
you can actually

00:48:59.570 --> 00:49:01.810 align:middle line:90%
bootstrap it and be fast.

00:49:01.810 --> 00:49:03.970 align:middle line:84%
Or if I want to relax
that constraint that I

00:49:03.970 --> 00:49:06.262 align:middle line:84%
have to have the same number
of calibration instruments

00:49:06.262 --> 00:49:09.090 align:middle line:84%
as points, maybe I'm going
to do a least squares fit.

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:12.850 align:middle line:84%
Again, just pick my favorite
minimizer and let it go and see

00:49:12.850 --> 00:49:15.170 align:middle line:90%
what we come up with.

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:17.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can also include
lots of other stuff

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:20.090 align:middle line:84%
in your objective function
other than just the errors.

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:22.925 align:middle line:84%
So one of the [INAUDIBLE]
we built with, for example,

00:49:22.925 --> 00:49:24.550 align:middle line:84%
are [INAUDIBLE]
smoothness constraints.

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:26.607 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
underspecify a curve.

00:49:26.607 --> 00:49:28.690 align:middle line:84%
But then I'm going to
impose additional smoothness

00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:30.732 align:middle line:84%
constraints because I want
my curve to be smooth.

00:49:30.732 --> 00:49:31.850 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:36.250 align:middle line:84%
I can also drop the exact
fit requirement a little bit.

00:49:36.250 --> 00:49:37.990 align:middle line:90%
There is no single price in it.

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:39.250 align:middle line:90%
There's always a bid offer.

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:41.010 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'm happy
with my curve prices

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:42.697 align:middle line:90%
things to within a bid offer.

00:49:42.697 --> 00:49:43.530 align:middle line:90%
That's close enough.

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:47.190 align:middle line:84%
I can't distinguish
that from an exact fit.

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:50.450 align:middle line:84%
And any number of other extra
constraints you can add here.

00:49:50.450 --> 00:49:53.858 align:middle line:90%


00:49:53.858 --> 00:49:55.150 align:middle line:90%
Now just one of the other ones.

00:49:55.150 --> 00:49:57.870 align:middle line:84%
And actually, when we
take a look at rates--

00:49:57.870 --> 00:50:00.090 align:middle line:84%
so this is SOFR rates
over the last, I think,

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:01.630 align:middle line:90%
three or four years--

00:50:01.630 --> 00:50:04.350 align:middle line:90%
this doesn't look very smooth.

00:50:04.350 --> 00:50:06.910 align:middle line:84%
This, in fact, looks
very stairsteppy.

00:50:06.910 --> 00:50:09.540 align:middle line:84%
And what you'll notice
here is the rates are--

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:11.790 align:middle line:84%
this is a little-- this is
an interesting spike-- cuts

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:14.350 align:middle line:84%
for tax, receipt issues, which
caused a lot of consternation

00:50:14.350 --> 00:50:15.517 align:middle line:90%
in the industry at the time.

00:50:15.517 --> 00:50:17.670 align:middle line:90%
But we'll ignore that for now.

00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:19.530 align:middle line:84%
It's generally flat,
and then it goes up.

00:50:19.530 --> 00:50:20.130 align:middle line:90%
It stays flat.

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:20.782 align:middle line:90%
It goes up.

00:50:20.782 --> 00:50:22.490 align:middle line:84%
These are the central
bank meeting dates.

00:50:22.490 --> 00:50:24.750 align:middle line:84%
So these are when the FOMC
gets together and decides

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:26.830 align:middle line:90%
we're increasing rates.

00:50:26.830 --> 00:50:29.070 align:middle line:84%
The daily rates will
generally stay constant

00:50:29.070 --> 00:50:32.370 align:middle line:90%
until the next FOMC rate change.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:35.130 align:middle line:84%
You can tell this graph
actually needs a 50 basis point

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:36.850 align:middle line:84%
drop-off of the
right side of that

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:39.410 align:middle line:84%
because they just
cut rates last week.

00:50:39.410 --> 00:50:43.588 align:middle line:84%
So this looks very like the
CDF function, for example.

00:50:43.588 --> 00:50:45.130 align:middle line:84%
So there's another
curve model that I

00:50:45.130 --> 00:50:48.490 align:middle line:90%
think is probably pretty much--

00:50:48.490 --> 00:50:51.490 align:middle line:84%
pretty widely used at this point
in the industry, which is I'm

00:50:51.490 --> 00:50:54.713 align:middle line:84%
going to build my knot points
based on the central bank

00:50:54.713 --> 00:50:57.130 align:middle line:84%
meeting dates, because those
are the only dates that rates

00:50:57.130 --> 00:51:00.250 align:middle line:84%
are going to change, in general,
or going to change materially.

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:01.900 align:middle line:84%
And I'll assume it's
flat in between.

00:51:01.900 --> 00:51:04.347 align:middle line:90%


00:51:04.347 --> 00:51:04.930 align:middle line:90%
So that's one.

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:07.080 align:middle line:84%
Again, I think that started
six or seven years ago.

00:51:07.080 --> 00:51:09.330 align:middle line:84%
And it's pretty-- far as I
know, almost everybody uses

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:11.523 align:middle line:84%
this methodology
now, where you have

00:51:11.523 --> 00:51:13.690 align:middle line:84%
generally the central bank
will publish the next two

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:14.988 align:middle line:90%
years meeting calendar.

00:51:14.988 --> 00:51:17.030 align:middle line:84%
If you really want to,
you can apply beyond that.

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:19.890 align:middle line:84%
But two years is about
the stuff you know.

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:23.987 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll assume,
say, a spline beyond that.

00:51:23.987 --> 00:51:25.570 align:middle line:84%
You end up with a
little bit of issues

00:51:25.570 --> 00:51:27.458 align:middle line:84%
on, how do you choose
your instruments?

00:51:27.458 --> 00:51:29.750 align:middle line:84%
There's eight meeting dates,
eight central bank meeting

00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:32.290 align:middle line:84%
dates a year, four
quarterly futures a year.

00:51:32.290 --> 00:51:34.770 align:middle line:84%
So you have a slight under
constraint issue here.

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:37.110 align:middle line:84%
So you need to fill
that in somehow.

00:51:37.110 --> 00:51:40.270 align:middle line:84%
You can include the one-year--
or sorry-- one-month futures,

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:41.530 align:middle line:90%
smoothness constraint.

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:43.090 align:middle line:90%
You have to do something else.

00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:45.390 align:middle line:90%
But that's fine.

00:51:45.390 --> 00:51:48.470 align:middle line:84%
Also recently, in the
last couple of years,

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:51.950 align:middle line:84%
actual swaps between meeting
dates have started to trade.

00:51:51.950 --> 00:51:53.870 align:middle line:84%
So basically, this is
a bet on the implied

00:51:53.870 --> 00:51:55.780 align:middle line:90%
rate after this meeting date.

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:57.530 align:middle line:84%
So if you think the
Fed is going to cut it

00:51:57.530 --> 00:52:00.830 align:middle line:84%
the next meeting, which I
think is early November,

00:52:00.830 --> 00:52:03.230 align:middle line:84%
you can buy a swap from
November through, I think, mid

00:52:03.230 --> 00:52:04.930 align:middle line:90%
or late December at this rate.

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:07.670 align:middle line:84%
And that's essentially the
implied rate after the meeting.

00:52:07.670 --> 00:52:08.670 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know
what the current--

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:10.253 align:middle line:84%
I think-- probably
thinking is they're

00:52:10.253 --> 00:52:11.610 align:middle line:90%
going to drop 25 basis points.

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:12.110 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:14.152 align:middle line:84%
They surprised me a little
bit by dropping to 50.

00:52:14.152 --> 00:52:14.930 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:18.630 align:middle line:84%
But there's instruments
that will directly

00:52:18.630 --> 00:52:20.570 align:middle line:84%
probe that and
reflect that rate.

00:52:20.570 --> 00:52:22.410 align:middle line:84%
So that's also
something you can do.

00:52:22.410 --> 00:52:26.067 align:middle line:90%


00:52:26.067 --> 00:52:27.650 align:middle line:84%
So I said, we can
make a hybrid curve,

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:30.025 align:middle line:90%
and this works pretty well.

00:52:30.025 --> 00:52:31.650 align:middle line:84%
Lots of other stuff
in curves, and this

00:52:31.650 --> 00:52:35.742 align:middle line:84%
is still a very active
area of research.

00:52:35.742 --> 00:52:37.450 align:middle line:84%
There's other features
that you sometimes

00:52:37.450 --> 00:52:40.410 align:middle line:84%
want to account to,
for example, turns

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:42.410 align:middle line:84%
is an interesting
feature where there's

00:52:42.410 --> 00:52:44.130 align:middle line:84%
dates that are
significant accounting

00:52:44.130 --> 00:52:45.783 align:middle line:90%
dates, year-ends, for example.

00:52:45.783 --> 00:52:48.450 align:middle line:84%
Generally, corporates will post
their earnings and their balance

00:52:48.450 --> 00:52:53.227 align:middle line:84%
sheets, return on assets,
on December 31 or quarterly.

00:52:53.227 --> 00:52:54.810 align:middle line:84%
So what that does
is it puts a premium

00:52:54.810 --> 00:52:56.470 align:middle line:90%
to borrow money over that rate.

00:52:56.470 --> 00:52:58.610 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a bank, I want to
minimize my balance sheet

00:52:58.610 --> 00:53:00.850 align:middle line:84%
when I'm reporting
what it is because I'm

00:53:00.850 --> 00:53:03.610 align:middle line:84%
going to be judged by the
investor community and things

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:07.010 align:middle line:84%
like return on assets, on total
assets, and things like that.

00:53:07.010 --> 00:53:10.010 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want my assets
to spike over year-end.

00:53:10.010 --> 00:53:11.508 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand,
money is money.

00:53:11.508 --> 00:53:13.050 align:middle line:84%
And if somebody
wants to borrow money

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:15.150 align:middle line:84%
over year-end for some
other reason, I'll do it.

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:17.890 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to charge
them a premium for that.

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:22.290 align:middle line:84%
So you find the
curves actually--

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:24.267 align:middle line:84%
if I actually look
at a curve, it

00:53:24.267 --> 00:53:25.850 align:middle line:84%
might be like doo,
doo, doo, doo, doo,

00:53:25.850 --> 00:53:28.690 align:middle line:84%
but then I'll have a
big spike every year.

00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:31.870 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that's one year,
where this essentially

00:53:31.870 --> 00:53:34.870 align:middle line:84%
is the premium for
borrowing over year-end.

00:53:34.870 --> 00:53:37.190 align:middle line:84%
So those you can include
in curves as well.

00:53:37.190 --> 00:53:40.070 align:middle line:84%
And you'll find some
of the hedge funds,

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:41.812 align:middle line:84%
in particular, are
slightly nasty.

00:53:41.812 --> 00:53:43.270 align:middle line:84%
So they'll do a
trade where they'll

00:53:43.270 --> 00:53:46.710 align:middle line:84%
want to do one swap to this date
and the second swap opposite

00:53:46.710 --> 00:53:48.230 align:middle line:90%
direction to that date.

00:53:48.230 --> 00:53:51.990 align:middle line:84%
So they're really just picking
out whatever curve-building

00:53:51.990 --> 00:53:53.630 align:middle line:90%
you're doing over year end.

00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:55.370 align:middle line:84%
And it's very easy
to get that wrong.

00:53:55.370 --> 00:53:57.460 align:middle line:84%
And you do it in enough
size, and it's real money.

00:53:57.460 --> 00:54:00.530 align:middle line:90%


00:54:00.530 --> 00:54:03.910 align:middle line:84%
And the other way they-- also,
other approaches you can do,

00:54:03.910 --> 00:54:06.070 align:middle line:90%
for example, do a PCA method.

00:54:06.070 --> 00:54:08.530 align:middle line:84%
One of the things on the
curve is I may build a curve.

00:54:08.530 --> 00:54:11.870 align:middle line:84%
Typically, it's about
50 inputs for $1 curve.

00:54:11.870 --> 00:54:14.580 align:middle line:84%
There's widely varying
liquidity among all those 50.

00:54:14.580 --> 00:54:16.830 align:middle line:84%
Typically, there's going to
be about a half dozen that

00:54:16.830 --> 00:54:18.830 align:middle line:90%
are the super liquid points.

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:22.390 align:middle line:84%
And then the rest of the stuff
will slowly become less liquid.

00:54:22.390 --> 00:54:24.960 align:middle line:84%
So you can, if you want,
say, look at historical data,

00:54:24.960 --> 00:54:26.420 align:middle line:84%
work out what my
factors are, pick

00:54:26.420 --> 00:54:28.500 align:middle line:84%
the first half dozen
factors which represent

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:30.300 align:middle line:90%
shapes of curve moves.

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Have those driven by
my most liquid stuff,

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:34.260 align:middle line:90%
and use that for a curve.

00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:36.600 align:middle line:84%
That works quite well in
bond world, for example.

00:54:36.600 --> 00:54:41.100 align:middle line:90%
So lots of other possibilities.

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:42.290 align:middle line:90%
Talk briefly about hedging.

00:54:42.290 --> 00:54:44.322 align:middle line:90%


00:54:44.322 --> 00:54:45.780 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things
you want to do

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:48.500 align:middle line:84%
if you're running a trading
book of some sort or asset

00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:52.140 align:middle line:84%
management or something, you
want to know, what is my risk?

00:54:52.140 --> 00:54:55.820 align:middle line:84%
And risk, generally, in the
trading desk view of the world

00:54:55.820 --> 00:55:00.380 align:middle line:84%
is, how much do I make or lose
if my, say, rates go up or down?

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:03.340 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to use just
simple PV01 is my main risk

00:55:03.340 --> 00:55:05.900 align:middle line:84%
measure, which is just
a sensitivity to one

00:55:05.900 --> 00:55:07.460 align:middle line:90%
basis-point increase.

00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:10.000 align:middle line:84%
Nothing too-- I've got a
two-sided difference here.

00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:13.360 align:middle line:84%
Just PV at rates plus a bit
minus PV at rates down a bit,

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:16.340 align:middle line:84%
normalized by two
times the size of that.

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:18.520 align:middle line:84%
Do this for each parameter,
and I get a delta strip.

00:55:18.520 --> 00:55:20.980 align:middle line:84%
And that's typically what
I'm going to use for hedging.

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:22.480 align:middle line:84%
That's what I'm
going to have limits

00:55:22.480 --> 00:55:23.962 align:middle line:90%
put on me from risk management.

00:55:23.962 --> 00:55:25.920 align:middle line:84%
That's what I'm going to
use, and an assortment

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:27.640 align:middle line:90%
of other things.

00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:28.700 align:middle line:90%
Very standard approach.

00:55:28.700 --> 00:55:31.492 align:middle line:84%
And you can do that for
any parameter you want.

00:55:31.492 --> 00:55:33.200 align:middle line:84%
You work out just what
is my sensitivity?

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:35.920 align:middle line:84%
By generally, a
brute-force bump in reval.

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:37.300 align:middle line:90%
Change the parameter reprice.

00:55:37.300 --> 00:55:38.300 align:middle line:90%
Take the difference.

00:55:38.300 --> 00:55:39.800 align:middle line:84%
You can get a bit
cleverer than that

00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:42.200 align:middle line:84%
if you want to go to
analytical hedge computations

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:47.640 align:middle line:84%
and things like that, but the
core method is still the same.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:49.240 align:middle line:90%
So that's first order.

00:55:49.240 --> 00:55:51.565 align:middle line:84%
I can also-- depending
on my product,

00:55:51.565 --> 00:55:53.440 align:middle line:84%
everything has got a
little bit of convexity.

00:55:53.440 --> 00:55:57.060 align:middle line:84%
And depending on what my trading
style is may or may not matter.

00:55:57.060 --> 00:56:00.640 align:middle line:84%
So I might want to do a gamma,
which is my second order risk.

00:56:00.640 --> 00:56:05.200 align:middle line:84%
Generally, the change in PV
squared by D rate squared.

00:56:05.200 --> 00:56:06.900 align:middle line:90%
Many definitions of gamma.

00:56:06.900 --> 00:56:09.300 align:middle line:84%
And at least in equity
world, it's a little simpler.

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:11.782 align:middle line:84%
In the interest rate world,
it's a little more annoying

00:56:11.782 --> 00:56:13.240 align:middle line:84%
because interest
rates, I said, you

00:56:13.240 --> 00:56:16.860 align:middle line:84%
have a yield curve that
might have 50 inputs on it.

00:56:16.860 --> 00:56:19.420 align:middle line:84%
So my delta will
have 50 computations.

00:56:19.420 --> 00:56:21.420 align:middle line:84%
Gamma is going to be that
squared because I need

00:56:21.420 --> 00:56:23.020 align:middle line:90%
to bump each of them in pairs.

00:56:23.020 --> 00:56:25.400 align:middle line:84%
2,500 computations
is very expensive,

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:27.000 align:middle line:84%
so you probably don't
want to do that.

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:29.460 align:middle line:84%
So again, you can do a
parallel gamma or factor

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:31.080 align:middle line:84%
gamma, a number of
other approaches,

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:32.940 align:middle line:90%
or just use scenario analysis.

00:56:32.940 --> 00:56:35.920 align:middle line:84%
Lots of ways to get your
higher order terms in there.

00:56:35.920 --> 00:56:41.260 align:middle line:90%


00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:43.640 align:middle line:84%
As soon as you start
actually hedging in practice,

00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:45.900 align:middle line:84%
you hit a whole lot
of practical issues

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:49.140 align:middle line:90%
that you had to be aware of.

00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:51.813 align:middle line:84%
How much is the higher
order terms contribute?

00:56:51.813 --> 00:56:54.480 align:middle line:84%
You can have third order terms,
fourth order terms, cross terms,

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:57.580 align:middle line:84%
and so on, all sorts
of weird complexities.

00:56:57.580 --> 00:56:59.260 align:middle line:90%
How do I choose my bump size?

00:56:59.260 --> 00:57:01.260 align:middle line:84%
The standard is a basis
point, but that's really

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:02.860 align:middle line:90%
just for reporting.

00:57:02.860 --> 00:57:05.220 align:middle line:84%
And this depends a little
bit on your numerical method

00:57:05.220 --> 00:57:07.300 align:middle line:90%
and what you're doing.

00:57:07.300 --> 00:57:08.800 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm in a
Monte Carlo world,

00:57:08.800 --> 00:57:10.640 align:middle line:84%
for example, I have a
Monte Carlo pricing engine,

00:57:10.640 --> 00:57:12.180 align:middle line:84%
I probably want to
bump a little bigger.

00:57:12.180 --> 00:57:13.640 align:middle line:84%
Because there's
Monte Carlo noise

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:15.080 align:middle line:90%
because it's random numbers.

00:57:15.080 --> 00:57:16.300 align:middle line:90%
So it's a little noisy.

00:57:16.300 --> 00:57:17.440 align:middle line:90%
So my bumps are too small.

00:57:17.440 --> 00:57:19.680 align:middle line:84%
My difference is going
to be quite small.

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:21.380 align:middle line:84%
Some of the other
methods, for example,

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:24.980 align:middle line:84%
some of the PDE-based methods,
if I'm doing a large bump,

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:26.820 align:middle line:84%
I can see grid effects
starting to hit it.

00:57:26.820 --> 00:57:28.920 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of
numerical issues here,

00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:31.805 align:middle line:84%
and everyone's a
little bit different.

00:57:31.805 --> 00:57:33.180 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
sequential bumps.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:35.287 align:middle line:84%
There's all sorts of
little convexities come in.

00:57:35.287 --> 00:57:37.620 align:middle line:84%
And I said, you're exposed
to your interpolation method.

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:39.140 align:middle line:84%
If I'm doing a
local interpolation,

00:57:39.140 --> 00:57:41.260 align:middle line:84%
my hedges are concentrated
around my maturity.

00:57:41.260 --> 00:57:42.677 align:middle line:84%
If I'm doing a
global one, they're

00:57:42.677 --> 00:57:43.920 align:middle line:90%
going to be more distributed.

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:45.600 align:middle line:84%
So this was something
that, again, you

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:48.100 align:middle line:84%
get into lots of arguments with
people about pretty quickly.

00:57:48.100 --> 00:57:50.480 align:middle line:90%


00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:53.040 align:middle line:84%
One of the other things to do,
depending on your jurisdiction

00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:58.080 align:middle line:84%
may be a regulatory requirement,
is to do your P&L attribution.

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:00.340 align:middle line:90%
So I come in the morning.

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:04.220 align:middle line:84%
I've got a set of positions, set
of whatever market is whatever.

00:58:04.220 --> 00:58:05.760 align:middle line:90%
At the end of the day I go home.

00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:07.960 align:middle line:84%
I've made or lost
some amount of money.

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:10.520 align:middle line:84%
So one of the key
questions is, why?

00:58:10.520 --> 00:58:12.000 align:middle line:90%
Where did that come from?

00:58:12.000 --> 00:58:13.075 align:middle line:90%
Am I a genius trader?

00:58:13.075 --> 00:58:14.200 align:middle line:90%
Did I get the market right?

00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:18.060 align:middle line:84%
Did I just have a lot of client
flow that I took a bid offer on?

00:58:18.060 --> 00:58:19.460 align:middle line:90%
What happened.

00:58:19.460 --> 00:58:22.500 align:middle line:84%
It's also a very good check on
how well your model is working,

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:24.620 align:middle line:90%
which is interesting.

00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:27.395 align:middle line:84%
So you know the P&L, I
said, from yesterday close

00:58:27.395 --> 00:58:29.520 align:middle line:84%
to today's close, because
you can value everything.

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:32.540 align:middle line:90%
So how did you get that?

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:34.728 align:middle line:84%
Generally, there's
three main components.

00:58:34.728 --> 00:58:37.020 align:middle line:84%
And this is also something
that you will have arguments

00:58:37.020 --> 00:58:39.062 align:middle line:84%
about until the [INAUDIBLE]
death of the universe

00:58:39.062 --> 00:58:41.260 align:middle line:84%
on, particularly on
the theta component.

00:58:41.260 --> 00:58:43.360 align:middle line:84%
You have a change
due to the portfolio.

00:58:43.360 --> 00:58:45.220 align:middle line:90%
So did I buy and sell trades?

00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:46.070 align:middle line:90%
Did I do unwinds?

00:58:46.070 --> 00:58:47.320 align:middle line:90%
Did things get assigned to me?

00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:48.280 align:middle line:90%
Do I have resets?

00:58:48.280 --> 00:58:50.020 align:middle line:90%
Did I exercise options?

00:58:50.020 --> 00:58:52.700 align:middle line:90%
All of that will have an impact.

00:58:52.700 --> 00:58:54.080 align:middle line:90%
There's the passage of time.

00:58:54.080 --> 00:58:55.620 align:middle line:90%
That's the complicated one.

00:58:55.620 --> 00:58:59.080 align:middle line:84%
So I went home yesterday,
and I came in today.

00:58:59.080 --> 00:59:00.120 align:middle line:90%
Market hasn't moved.

00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:03.300 align:middle line:84%
But what does market
not move actually mean?

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:07.180 align:middle line:84%
It's a very subtle effect
because interest rate curves are

00:59:07.180 --> 00:59:10.200 align:middle line:90%
a function of time versus this.

00:59:10.200 --> 00:59:15.160 align:middle line:84%
So when I move this date to that
date, what do I hold constant,

00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:16.520 align:middle line:90%
and what do I let change?

00:59:16.520 --> 00:59:19.800 align:middle line:84%
And there's more than one
intellectually defensible answer

00:59:19.800 --> 00:59:21.720 align:middle line:90%
to do this.

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:23.440 align:middle line:84%
So anytime I tell
people you do--

00:59:23.440 --> 00:59:24.660 align:middle line:90%
be very precise.

00:59:24.660 --> 00:59:26.640 align:middle line:84%
Make sure you know
exactly what you're doing.

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:28.780 align:middle line:84%
And again, there's lots
of defensible answers,

00:59:28.780 --> 00:59:30.845 align:middle line:84%
but you all have to
agree on your notation.

00:59:30.845 --> 00:59:32.720 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, there's
the more common one

00:59:32.720 --> 00:59:36.040 align:middle line:84%
that people think about, which
is, how does the market move?

00:59:36.040 --> 00:59:37.740 align:middle line:90%
So I'm long a billion bonds.

00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:40.120 align:middle line:90%
I got a PV01 of--

00:59:40.120 --> 00:59:42.520 align:middle line:84%
what is that-- $10 million
and one, or whatever.

00:59:42.520 --> 00:59:43.780 align:middle line:90%
Rates move five basis points.

00:59:43.780 --> 00:59:45.060 align:middle line:90%
I made $50 million.

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:46.082 align:middle line:90%
Well done, me.

00:59:46.082 --> 00:59:47.540 align:middle line:84%
Rates went down
three basis points.

00:59:47.540 --> 00:59:48.600 align:middle line:90%
I lost $30 million.

00:59:48.600 --> 00:59:50.720 align:middle line:90%
Less well done, me.

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:52.162 align:middle line:90%
So market impacts easily.

00:59:52.162 --> 00:59:54.620 align:middle line:84%
And I said, the portfolio change
is also one of these ones.

00:59:54.620 --> 00:59:56.680 align:middle line:84%
It's mechanically just
a pain in the ass.

00:59:56.680 --> 00:59:59.080 align:middle line:84%
You get introduced to
booking systems and all

00:59:59.080 --> 01:00:00.560 align:middle line:84%
your operational
flows and things

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:04.960 align:middle line:84%
like that, which can
confuse things a lot.

01:00:04.960 --> 01:00:08.260 align:middle line:84%
Finally, just still
bouncing between subjects,

01:00:08.260 --> 01:00:10.000 align:middle line:84%
so we still got a
few more minutes.

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:15.390 align:middle line:84%
Clearing is another thing
that came in post-2008.

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:19.310 align:middle line:84%
One of the features
in 2008 is people

01:00:19.310 --> 01:00:22.790 align:middle line:84%
became acutely aware of
their counterparty exposure.

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:26.290 align:middle line:84%
So I do a trade with you, and
we do it at 0PV initially.

01:00:26.290 --> 01:00:27.130 align:middle line:90%
Time passes.

01:00:27.130 --> 01:00:28.930 align:middle line:84%
Now it's worth $10
million to you.

01:00:28.930 --> 01:00:29.990 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:31.290 align:middle line:90%
This is not realized money.

01:00:31.290 --> 01:00:33.210 align:middle line:84%
This is money I owe
you in the future.

01:00:33.210 --> 01:00:35.390 align:middle line:84%
If I go bankrupt,
you lose $10 million.

01:00:35.390 --> 01:00:38.450 align:middle line:84%
So people become very aware
of-- and if you go bankrupt,

01:00:38.450 --> 01:00:40.830 align:middle line:84%
I get a $10 million
freebie, assuming

01:00:40.830 --> 01:00:42.930 align:middle line:84%
I can argue with your
bankruptcy judge.

01:00:42.930 --> 01:00:43.430 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:00:43.430 --> 01:00:46.052 align:middle line:84%
So there's people are
very acutely aware,

01:00:46.052 --> 01:00:47.510 align:middle line:84%
and Lehman really
brought that home

01:00:47.510 --> 01:00:49.470 align:middle line:90%
because they were a huge dealer.

01:00:49.470 --> 01:00:52.870 align:middle line:84%
They had many, many billions,
hundreds of billions

01:00:52.870 --> 01:00:55.550 align:middle line:84%
of dollars of
derivatives with people.

01:00:55.550 --> 01:00:57.930 align:middle line:84%
It, by the way, is why JP
Morgan is doing so well now

01:00:57.930 --> 01:01:00.347 align:middle line:84%
because they were the only
bank still standing in the fall

01:01:00.347 --> 01:01:00.910 align:middle line:90%
of '08.

01:01:00.910 --> 01:01:03.630 align:middle line:84%
So people had to replace an
enormous amount of Lehman

01:01:03.630 --> 01:01:07.230 align:middle line:84%
hedges, and JP was the only
one still actively trading.

01:01:07.230 --> 01:01:08.290 align:middle line:90%
Goldman's was in trouble.

01:01:08.290 --> 01:01:09.670 align:middle line:90%
Stanley was in trouble.

01:01:09.670 --> 01:01:12.470 align:middle line:84%
Bank of America, where I
was, had just bought Merrill.

01:01:12.470 --> 01:01:15.050 align:middle line:90%
Merrill went bust.

01:01:15.050 --> 01:01:17.410 align:middle line:84%
All the-- Citigroup
was having all sorts

01:01:17.410 --> 01:01:21.210 align:middle line:84%
of issues with guaranteed
credit exposures and things.

01:01:21.210 --> 01:01:23.090 align:middle line:90%
So JP Morgan just made out.

01:01:23.090 --> 01:01:25.130 align:middle line:84%
The bid offers went up
by about a factor of 20

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:29.570 align:middle line:84%
for a month or two there, And
They just raked it all in.

01:01:29.570 --> 01:01:33.030 align:middle line:84%
Good for them, I guess, but
not good for anybody else.

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:35.730 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things
the regulators decided

01:01:35.730 --> 01:01:38.090 align:middle line:84%
after that was people
would have to use clearing

01:01:38.090 --> 01:01:39.970 align:middle line:90%
for vanilla swaps.

01:01:39.970 --> 01:01:42.730 align:middle line:84%
The clearing is a concept that's
been around in futures forever,

01:01:42.730 --> 01:01:45.010 align:middle line:84%
but in swaps it's
relatively new.

01:01:45.010 --> 01:01:47.395 align:middle line:84%
So what that means is, say,
I'll do a trade with you,

01:01:47.395 --> 01:01:48.270 align:middle line:90%
and we'll do a trade.

01:01:48.270 --> 01:01:48.770 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:01:48.770 --> 01:01:50.253 align:middle line:90%
You guys, we agree on the terms.

01:01:50.253 --> 01:01:52.170 align:middle line:84%
We will then both assign
our side of the trade

01:01:52.170 --> 01:01:56.690 align:middle line:84%
to a central clearing
house, say, a CME and LCH.

01:01:56.690 --> 01:01:59.930 align:middle line:84%
London Clearinghouse and the
CME are the two big ones.

01:01:59.930 --> 01:02:02.687 align:middle line:84%
So then both of us now
face, say, the CME.

01:02:02.687 --> 01:02:04.770 align:middle line:84%
And you and I are now
separated, and we don't care

01:02:04.770 --> 01:02:06.030 align:middle line:90%
what happens to each other.

01:02:06.030 --> 01:02:09.750 align:middle line:84%
You can go bust, and I'll be
sad, but I'm not losing money.

01:02:09.750 --> 01:02:12.090 align:middle line:90%
So that came in.

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:15.470 align:middle line:84%
And clearinghouses
are generally big.

01:02:15.470 --> 01:02:17.830 align:middle line:84%
One of the worries
is we've traded off

01:02:17.830 --> 01:02:20.950 align:middle line:84%
small and large
potential disasters

01:02:20.950 --> 01:02:24.150 align:middle line:84%
for a very small number
of enormous disasters.

01:02:24.150 --> 01:02:27.470 align:middle line:84%
If LCH goes bust, it's
time to get guns and ammo

01:02:27.470 --> 01:02:29.030 align:middle line:84%
and head for the
hills because it's

01:02:29.030 --> 01:02:31.530 align:middle line:84%
going to be just a complete
disaster in the financial world.

01:02:31.530 --> 01:02:34.070 align:middle line:84%
They are so big and so
integrated with everything.

01:02:34.070 --> 01:02:35.870 align:middle line:84%
And all the members
post various margins

01:02:35.870 --> 01:02:37.090 align:middle line:90%
to them and membership fees.

01:02:37.090 --> 01:02:38.650 align:middle line:90%
So they're pretty robust.

01:02:38.650 --> 01:02:40.710 align:middle line:84%
But if something
did weird happen,

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:45.650 align:middle line:84%
it would be quite
unpleasant there.

01:02:45.650 --> 01:02:47.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, one of the things
about the clearinghouses

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:50.430 align:middle line:84%
is that dealers are
required, but you end up

01:02:50.430 --> 01:02:53.990 align:middle line:84%
with this now situation
where $1 I'm paying to LCH

01:02:53.990 --> 01:02:57.230 align:middle line:84%
is not the same as $1
I'm paying to the CME.

01:02:57.230 --> 01:02:59.670 align:middle line:84%
These are not
fungible amounts, even

01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:02.470 align:middle line:90%
though they're both dollars.

01:03:02.470 --> 01:03:04.710 align:middle line:84%
So it's quite interesting
because I can't-- if I have

01:03:04.710 --> 01:03:10.250 align:middle line:84%
a swap with LCH, and I swap with
CME, I can't offset these two.

01:03:10.250 --> 01:03:12.610 align:middle line:84%
I can in my books, but not
in terms of operationally

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:14.210 align:middle line:90%
and collateral and everything.

01:03:14.210 --> 01:03:16.715 align:middle line:84%
So what happened probably
eight or nine years ago,

01:03:16.715 --> 01:03:18.090 align:middle line:84%
it was quite a
significant spread

01:03:18.090 --> 01:03:19.930 align:middle line:90%
opened up between these two.

01:03:19.930 --> 01:03:22.030 align:middle line:90%
So this is from 2017.

01:03:22.030 --> 01:03:24.810 align:middle line:84%
I guess Tradition is
one of the big brokers.

01:03:24.810 --> 01:03:26.370 align:middle line:84%
This is the difference
between if I

01:03:26.370 --> 01:03:30.167 align:middle line:84%
want to pay fixed on CME
versus pay fixed on LCH.

01:03:30.167 --> 01:03:31.750 align:middle line:84%
So again, if I'm
doing a 30-year swap,

01:03:31.750 --> 01:03:34.262 align:middle line:84%
it's now 3 and 1/2 basis
points between them.

01:03:34.262 --> 01:03:35.970 align:middle line:84%
And again, this is a
market where the bid

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:38.690 align:middle line:90%
offer is 0.2 of a basis point.

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:41.850 align:middle line:84%
So the question is, why did
this-- and this persisted

01:03:41.850 --> 01:03:42.550 align:middle line:90%
for a long time.

01:03:42.550 --> 01:03:44.050 align:middle line:84%
It's actually flipped
sign recently.

01:03:44.050 --> 01:03:45.850 align:middle line:90%
But this was around for years.

01:03:45.850 --> 01:03:47.770 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to guess
why this kind of-- no.

01:03:47.770 --> 01:03:49.690 align:middle line:84%
Why can't I just
arbitrage this away?

01:03:49.690 --> 01:03:51.110 align:middle line:90%
I'll go receive fix in CME.

01:03:51.110 --> 01:03:53.770 align:middle line:84%
I'll pay fix in LCH, and I'll
collect 3 and 1/2 basis points

01:03:53.770 --> 01:03:56.170 align:middle line:84%
and go buy my island
in the Caribbean.

01:03:56.170 --> 01:03:58.450 align:middle line:90%
Why can I not do that?

01:03:58.450 --> 01:04:01.810 align:middle line:90%
Or why did nobody do that?

01:04:01.810 --> 01:04:04.992 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to
hazard a guess even?

01:04:04.992 --> 01:04:05.950 align:middle line:90%
It's quite interesting.

01:04:05.950 --> 01:04:09.890 align:middle line:84%
And it's one of the pretty core
things in a post '08 world,

01:04:09.890 --> 01:04:11.340 align:middle line:90%
which makes things a lot harder.

01:04:11.340 --> 01:04:14.710 align:middle line:90%


01:04:14.710 --> 01:04:16.450 align:middle line:90%
No guesses?

01:04:16.450 --> 01:04:16.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:04:16.950 --> 01:04:18.630 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Like a zero spread.

01:04:18.630 --> 01:04:18.930 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Pardon?

01:04:18.930 --> 01:04:20.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Like a zero spread.

01:04:20.430 --> 01:04:22.150 align:middle line:84%
Like, why are
[INAUDIBLE] something.

01:04:22.150 --> 01:04:25.055 align:middle line:84%
Like, you receive versus on
your short, it's different.

01:04:25.055 --> 01:04:26.930 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: But
why does that persist?

01:04:26.930 --> 01:04:27.790 align:middle line:90%
Why can't I just--

01:04:27.790 --> 01:04:30.410 align:middle line:84%
there's enough people out there
that are happy to make money.

01:04:30.410 --> 01:04:32.053 align:middle line:84%
Why can't they
just take that out?

01:04:32.053 --> 01:04:34.470 align:middle line:84%
If you told me I could make 3
and 1/2 basis points running

01:04:34.470 --> 01:04:36.832 align:middle line:84%
for 30 years, I'll
do that all day,

01:04:36.832 --> 01:04:38.290 align:middle line:84%
and that'll make
me a ton of money.

01:04:38.290 --> 01:04:41.950 align:middle line:90%


01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:44.950 align:middle line:90%
There's a couple of reasons.

01:04:44.950 --> 01:04:47.310 align:middle line:84%
You have to post
margin on both sides.

01:04:47.310 --> 01:04:49.330 align:middle line:84%
The clearinghouse, as
part of doing clearing,

01:04:49.330 --> 01:04:50.850 align:middle line:90%
require you post first swap.

01:04:50.850 --> 01:04:52.970 align:middle line:84%
It's typically a percent
or 2% of the notional.

01:04:52.970 --> 01:04:55.010 align:middle line:84%
So you have to post money
to them on both sides.

01:04:55.010 --> 01:04:56.470 align:middle line:84%
You're now double
posting margin.

01:04:56.470 --> 01:04:59.590 align:middle line:84%
That costs you, and that's
the main driver of it.

01:04:59.590 --> 01:05:01.830 align:middle line:84%
Some of the other features
are other products trade

01:05:01.830 --> 01:05:04.210 align:middle line:84%
on these clearinghouses,
and you can net them.

01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:08.090 align:middle line:84%
For example, CME is the
big futures counterparty.

01:05:08.090 --> 01:05:10.783 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm long a ton of interest
rate futures and short a ton

01:05:10.783 --> 01:05:12.450 align:middle line:84%
of swaps, I get at
least partial netting

01:05:12.450 --> 01:05:14.410 align:middle line:90%
in my margin between the two.

01:05:14.410 --> 01:05:18.610 align:middle line:84%
Then there's a couple of
non, less economic things.

01:05:18.610 --> 01:05:21.690 align:middle line:84%
This thing was largely driven
by some very large US asset

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:24.030 align:middle line:84%
managers, people like
PIMCO, who is ginormous,

01:05:24.030 --> 01:05:28.490 align:middle line:84%
that wanted to pay fixed all day
to hedge their loan portfolio.

01:05:28.490 --> 01:05:31.410 align:middle line:84%
They are a little less
trusting of the LCH

01:05:31.410 --> 01:05:34.290 align:middle line:84%
because the LCH is owned
by a consortium of dealers.

01:05:34.290 --> 01:05:35.910 align:middle line:90%
CME is owned a private company.

01:05:35.910 --> 01:05:37.970 align:middle line:84%
So their thought
was the LCH is going

01:05:37.970 --> 01:05:40.930 align:middle line:84%
to be more favorable to dealers
because the dealers own them.

01:05:40.930 --> 01:05:44.890 align:middle line:84%
So if it comes down to a
legal issue or something,

01:05:44.890 --> 01:05:46.498 align:middle line:90%
they're going to be biased.

01:05:46.498 --> 01:05:48.790 align:middle line:84%
The other one is they want
it to be in US jurisdiction.

01:05:48.790 --> 01:05:51.250 align:middle line:84%
LCH is in London,
L being London.

01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:52.370 align:middle line:90%
And they just want--

01:05:52.370 --> 01:05:54.650 align:middle line:84%
they want to deal with
the legal issues in the US

01:05:54.650 --> 01:05:56.010 align:middle line:84%
because that's where
they have their expertise

01:05:56.010 --> 01:05:56.910 align:middle line:90%
and where they're based.

01:05:56.910 --> 01:05:57.870 align:middle line:90%
So a couple of things.

01:05:57.870 --> 01:05:59.120 align:middle line:90%
Again, it's quite interesting.

01:05:59.120 --> 01:06:00.830 align:middle line:84%
But one of the things
this introduces,

01:06:00.830 --> 01:06:03.360 align:middle line:84%
the fundamental thing, is
that because of the margining

01:06:03.360 --> 01:06:05.318 align:middle line:84%
requirements,
there's a spread here

01:06:05.318 --> 01:06:07.360 align:middle line:84%
that can exist between
things that would normally

01:06:07.360 --> 01:06:09.080 align:middle line:90%
be arbitrageable.

01:06:09.080 --> 01:06:10.100 align:middle line:90%
And it can persist.

01:06:10.100 --> 01:06:12.360 align:middle line:90%
Fx has all sorts of these, too.

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:14.600 align:middle line:84%
And this is coming to the
point that it actually

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:17.040 align:middle line:84%
to correctly and completely
calculate the prices,

01:06:17.040 --> 01:06:18.880 align:middle line:84%
is if you need to
include a lot of things

01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:21.808 align:middle line:84%
outside of the core
pricing of the instrument,

01:06:21.808 --> 01:06:23.100 align:middle line:90%
how are you going to margin it?

01:06:23.100 --> 01:06:24.420 align:middle line:84%
What capital do you
have to post it?

01:06:24.420 --> 01:06:25.880 align:middle line:84%
Or is there any
other kind of things

01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:27.380 align:middle line:84%
like this that are
going to cost you

01:06:27.380 --> 01:06:29.080 align:middle line:84%
money that have
not traditionally

01:06:29.080 --> 01:06:31.600 align:middle line:90%
been accounted for?

01:06:31.600 --> 01:06:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And I would say one of the huge
quant efforts over the last 15

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:37.800 align:middle line:84%
years is to work out what
are all these extra costs,

01:06:37.800 --> 01:06:40.880 align:middle line:84%
and how do I push them back
into the upfront pricing?

01:06:40.880 --> 01:06:44.360 align:middle line:84%
If I do a swap for a very
small P&L with a counterparty,

01:06:44.360 --> 01:06:47.120 align:middle line:84%
and then it turns out I have to
post an enormous margin that's

01:06:47.120 --> 01:06:49.060 align:middle line:84%
going to cost me
way more than that,

01:06:49.060 --> 01:06:50.795 align:middle line:84%
I've just done a
money-losing trade.

01:06:50.795 --> 01:06:51.920 align:middle line:90%
So I don't want to do that.

01:06:51.920 --> 01:06:54.800 align:middle line:84%
You can't do too many of those,
or you're not trading anymore.

01:06:54.800 --> 01:06:57.620 align:middle line:84%
So you have to correctly account
for all of these extra costs,

01:06:57.620 --> 01:06:59.720 align:middle line:84%
and that's quite
non-trivial, just

01:06:59.720 --> 01:07:02.380 align:middle line:84%
both mechanically in
their system side,

01:07:02.380 --> 01:07:03.920 align:middle line:90%
as well as conceptually.

01:07:03.920 --> 01:07:06.220 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of these things
are portfolio effects.

01:07:06.220 --> 01:07:08.420 align:middle line:84%
So how do I then allocate
that to individual trades

01:07:08.420 --> 01:07:10.310 align:middle line:84%
is not necessarily
super obvious.

01:07:10.310 --> 01:07:16.900 align:middle line:90%


01:07:16.900 --> 01:07:17.400 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:07:17.400 --> 01:07:19.180 align:middle line:84%
Coming to the end at
about the right time,

01:07:19.180 --> 01:07:23.070 align:middle line:84%
quick blurb about electronic
trading, which is--

01:07:23.070 --> 01:07:25.320 align:middle line:84%
if any of you guys get into
this area in your careers,

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:30.443 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the very quite
interesting, very hot areas.

01:07:30.443 --> 01:07:31.860 align:middle line:84%
Electronic trading
has been around

01:07:31.860 --> 01:07:34.500 align:middle line:90%
in equities for quite a while.

01:07:34.500 --> 01:07:36.860 align:middle line:84%
My standard metric is
equities is probably

01:07:36.860 --> 01:07:38.700 align:middle line:84%
10 years ahead of foreign
exchange, which is

01:07:38.700 --> 01:07:40.700 align:middle line:90%
10 years ahead of fixed income.

01:07:40.700 --> 01:07:44.020 align:middle line:84%
And one of the graphs there is
from one of the data providers--

01:07:44.020 --> 01:07:47.420 align:middle line:84%
the percentage of markets
in some of the fixed income

01:07:47.420 --> 01:07:51.250 align:middle line:84%
products that are
traded electronically--

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:51.940 align:middle line:90%
two, basically.

01:07:51.940 --> 01:07:53.780 align:middle line:84%
There's electronic trading,
which I set on my keyboard,

01:07:53.780 --> 01:07:54.500 align:middle line:90%
and I go.

01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:55.600 align:middle line:90%
Computer does everything.

01:07:55.600 --> 01:07:58.260 align:middle line:84%
Or I can pick up the phone,
and I can call somebody-- voice

01:07:58.260 --> 01:08:00.240 align:middle line:90%
trading.

01:08:00.240 --> 01:08:02.000 align:middle line:84%
So again, there's
a spectrum here.

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:04.560 align:middle line:84%
On the right, I've got
the very liquid stuff--

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:06.380 align:middle line:84%
US equity products,
cash equities.

01:08:06.380 --> 01:08:09.440 align:middle line:90%
Those are all 90%-plus Fx spot--

01:08:09.440 --> 01:08:11.600 align:middle line:84%
all traded
electronically, basically.

01:08:11.600 --> 01:08:13.600 align:middle line:84%
And then I start to
get less and less.

01:08:13.600 --> 01:08:15.400 align:middle line:84%
European governments,
US governments--

01:08:15.400 --> 01:08:16.939 align:middle line:90%
both predominantly electronic.

01:08:16.939 --> 01:08:21.680 align:middle line:84%
But we're talking 60% to
80% of the market here.

01:08:21.680 --> 01:08:24.120 align:middle line:84%
What you tend to see is a
lot of the smaller trades

01:08:24.120 --> 01:08:26.939 align:middle line:84%
we'll go through electronically
for a couple of reasons.

01:08:26.939 --> 01:08:28.700 align:middle line:84%
These are not market
moving trades.

01:08:28.700 --> 01:08:31.000 align:middle line:84%
So people are happy to let
the machine do its thing.

01:08:31.000 --> 01:08:33.742 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm buying $1
million of a Treasury,

01:08:33.742 --> 01:08:36.200 align:middle line:84%
that's not going to affect a
market that trades $1 trillion

01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:37.080 align:middle line:90%
a day.

01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:39.319 align:middle line:84%
If I'm buying $100
million, maybe

01:08:39.319 --> 01:08:40.736 align:middle line:84%
if I'm buying $500
million, I need

01:08:40.736 --> 01:08:43.027 align:middle line:84%
to be careful because that
is going to move the market,

01:08:43.027 --> 01:08:45.000 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to move
the market against me,

01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:46.458 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to
end up paying more

01:08:46.458 --> 01:08:49.152 align:middle line:90%
than I would otherwise do that.

01:08:49.152 --> 01:08:50.819 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing
is I get down there.

01:08:50.819 --> 01:08:53.020 align:middle line:84%
So I have some of the
investment-grade corporates,

01:08:53.020 --> 01:08:54.200 align:middle line:90%
high-yield bonds.

01:08:54.200 --> 01:08:56.740 align:middle line:84%
And I get down to the left,
which is really not electronic.

01:08:56.740 --> 01:09:00.382 align:middle line:84%
It's things like emerging
market bonds and municipals.

01:09:00.382 --> 01:09:01.840 align:middle line:84%
Again, munis is
one of these ones--

01:09:01.840 --> 01:09:04.100 align:middle line:90%
quite an interesting market.

01:09:04.100 --> 01:09:07.620 align:middle line:84%
Before I was at Mizuho, I
was at TD, Toronto-Dominion.

01:09:07.620 --> 01:09:10.140 align:middle line:84%
We acquired a muni bond
electronic trading firm

01:09:10.140 --> 01:09:13.580 align:middle line:84%
from Chicago, which was
tremendously successful.

01:09:13.580 --> 01:09:16.460 align:middle line:84%
And they have about half of
the electronic market in munis.

01:09:16.460 --> 01:09:19.460 align:middle line:84%
So munis is something that
there are huge number of bonds.

01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:21.620 align:middle line:84%
There's something like
3.5 million issuers

01:09:21.620 --> 01:09:26.060 align:middle line:84%
in the country, which is the
states, the towns, bridge

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.460 align:middle line:84%
projects, school boards,
all sorts of things

01:09:29.460 --> 01:09:31.140 align:middle line:90%
that issue municipal bonds.

01:09:31.140 --> 01:09:33.740 align:middle line:90%
They're all a little different.

01:09:33.740 --> 01:09:35.899 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of
nonstandardization

01:09:35.899 --> 01:09:37.845 align:middle line:90%
in the actual bond structures.

01:09:37.845 --> 01:09:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing,
there's a tax advantage.

01:09:39.720 --> 01:09:40.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you
guys-- any of you

01:09:40.700 --> 01:09:43.200 align:middle line:84%
guys have a PA at this point,
or hopefully in the future,

01:09:43.200 --> 01:09:45.100 align:middle line:90%
not too much longer.

01:09:45.100 --> 01:09:47.700 align:middle line:84%
Muni bonds, you don't pay
federal income tax on them,

01:09:47.700 --> 01:09:50.899 align:middle line:84%
which means typically,
the yields are about 70%

01:09:50.899 --> 01:09:53.100 align:middle line:84%
of a taxable issue because
you get the same net

01:09:53.100 --> 01:09:54.460 align:middle line:90%
after tax yield.

01:09:54.460 --> 01:09:56.180 align:middle line:90%
This means it's a retail market.

01:09:56.180 --> 01:09:59.000 align:middle line:84%
Institutional investors
don't get the tax break.

01:09:59.000 --> 01:10:00.960 align:middle line:84%
So they are less
interested in munis.

01:10:00.960 --> 01:10:03.400 align:middle line:84%
Retail investors typically
are not buying things

01:10:03.400 --> 01:10:04.660 align:middle line:90%
in blocks of 100 million.

01:10:04.660 --> 01:10:07.420 align:middle line:84%
They're buying things in
blocks of $5,000 or $10,000,

01:10:07.420 --> 01:10:08.640 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

01:10:08.640 --> 01:10:10.740 align:middle line:84%
So the average trade
size is very small.

01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:14.280 align:middle line:84%
So at TD, they did something
like 5,000 to 10,000 trades

01:10:14.280 --> 01:10:14.780 align:middle line:90%
a day.

01:10:14.780 --> 01:10:17.640 align:middle line:84%
But the average trade
size, I think, was $28,000,

01:10:17.640 --> 01:10:19.620 align:middle line:84%
which in a government
market is absurd.

01:10:19.620 --> 01:10:21.140 align:middle line:90%
That's not even an odd lot.

01:10:21.140 --> 01:10:23.598 align:middle line:84%
That's something the janitor
picks up at the end of the day

01:10:23.598 --> 01:10:26.280 align:middle line:90%
and just does something with.

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:27.620 align:middle line:90%
So it's quite interesting.

01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:28.740 align:middle line:84%
But on the other hand,
you do enough of it,

01:10:28.740 --> 01:10:30.200 align:middle line:90%
you can still make good money.

01:10:30.200 --> 01:10:32.000 align:middle line:84%
Emerging markets is
another one where

01:10:32.000 --> 01:10:34.160 align:middle line:84%
the bonds are very
idiosyncratic,

01:10:34.160 --> 01:10:37.060 align:middle line:84%
and there's a lot of
very special stuff.

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:39.900 align:middle line:84%
There's not really a
generic Chilean bond.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:42.220 align:middle line:84%
You have to know exactly
what it is, how is it paid,

01:10:42.220 --> 01:10:44.260 align:middle line:84%
what's the credit
support, all of that.

01:10:44.260 --> 01:10:46.640 align:middle line:84%
So these things are
generally done over the phone

01:10:46.640 --> 01:10:50.520 align:middle line:84%
with somebody because not
for the faint of heart,

01:10:50.520 --> 01:10:53.480 align:middle line:90%
though, I would say.

01:10:53.480 --> 01:10:57.380 align:middle line:84%
But as time goes on, things
are slowly moving up here,

01:10:57.380 --> 01:10:59.740 align:middle line:84%
and things are becoming
more electronic.

01:10:59.740 --> 01:11:01.520 align:middle line:84%
Electronic is
just-- it's faster.

01:11:01.520 --> 01:11:03.740 align:middle line:90%
It's cheaper.

01:11:03.740 --> 01:11:06.492 align:middle line:84%
You're less sensitive to
people taking advantage of you.

01:11:06.492 --> 01:11:07.200 align:middle line:90%
You have markets.

01:11:07.200 --> 01:11:09.340 align:middle line:90%
You can see where the market is.

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:13.380 align:middle line:84%
There are quite a variety
of participants here.

01:11:13.380 --> 01:11:15.300 align:middle line:90%
You have the trading shops.

01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:18.580 align:middle line:84%
There's people like XTX or Jane
Street or Hudson River Trading

01:11:18.580 --> 01:11:20.340 align:middle line:90%
or folks like that.

01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:21.720 align:middle line:90%
They don't have customers.

01:11:21.720 --> 01:11:23.320 align:middle line:84%
They just trade on
their own account.

01:11:23.320 --> 01:11:24.540 align:middle line:90%
They're very good.

01:11:24.540 --> 01:11:26.220 align:middle line:90%
They're very fast.

01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:28.460 align:middle line:84%
They generally make
markets in a lot of this.

01:11:28.460 --> 01:11:30.868 align:middle line:84%
Telling Peter a story
I had at some point.

01:11:30.868 --> 01:11:32.660 align:middle line:84%
I was at a Thanksgiving
dinner or something

01:11:32.660 --> 01:11:33.965 align:middle line:90%
with a big group of people.

01:11:33.965 --> 01:11:35.340 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
other people there

01:11:35.340 --> 01:11:38.220 align:middle line:84%
had just graduated from
college with a PhD in physics

01:11:38.220 --> 01:11:40.840 align:middle line:84%
in microwave guides, and
he'd been employed, I think,

01:11:40.840 --> 01:11:44.740 align:middle line:84%
by Jump Trading to work
out the optimal microwave

01:11:44.740 --> 01:11:47.636 align:middle line:84%
transmission to reduce
Chicago-New York latency.

01:11:47.636 --> 01:11:49.886 align:middle line:84%
Because Chicago is where the
exchanges are, and here's

01:11:49.886 --> 01:11:50.720 align:middle line:90%
where the customers are.

01:11:50.720 --> 01:11:53.050 align:middle line:84%
And I just remember talking to
that guy and thinking, Jesus--

01:11:53.050 --> 01:11:54.967 align:middle line:84%
I think I was at Bank
of America at the time--

01:11:54.967 --> 01:11:56.810 align:middle line:84%
we are never getting
in front of this.

01:11:56.810 --> 01:11:58.170 align:middle line:90%
These guys are just--

01:11:58.170 --> 01:11:59.890 align:middle line:84%
they are so advanced
in this stuff

01:11:59.890 --> 01:12:03.930 align:middle line:84%
that you have to be very careful
to pick what you're doing.

01:12:03.930 --> 01:12:06.230 align:middle line:84%
So these guys are
super fast, super good.

01:12:06.230 --> 01:12:07.710 align:middle line:84%
This is the
high-frequency traders,

01:12:07.710 --> 01:12:09.010 align:middle line:90%
people like [INAUDIBLE].

01:12:09.010 --> 01:12:10.810 align:middle line:84%
Then you have the
dealers, which are

01:12:10.810 --> 01:12:12.290 align:middle line:84%
people like the
Bank of Americas,

01:12:12.290 --> 01:12:15.410 align:middle line:90%
or TDs, Morgan Stanleys.

01:12:15.410 --> 01:12:17.557 align:middle line:90%
They generally are trading.

01:12:17.557 --> 01:12:18.390 align:middle line:90%
They're pretty good.

01:12:18.390 --> 01:12:19.150 align:middle line:90%
They're fast.

01:12:19.150 --> 01:12:21.730 align:middle line:84%
They're not building their
own microwave towers.

01:12:21.730 --> 01:12:22.790 align:middle line:90%
So they're doing that.

01:12:22.790 --> 01:12:23.690 align:middle line:90%
They have customers.

01:12:23.690 --> 01:12:25.495 align:middle line:90%
Customers are good.

01:12:25.495 --> 01:12:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Customers want to
trade with you.

01:12:26.870 --> 01:12:29.370 align:middle line:84%
The customers will pay you a
bid offer to do trade with you.

01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:31.650 align:middle line:90%
So you like that.

01:12:31.650 --> 01:12:35.810 align:middle line:84%
And there are of low
latency, but microseconds

01:12:35.810 --> 01:12:36.833 align:middle line:90%
and not nanoseconds.

01:12:36.833 --> 01:12:38.250 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
the customers who

01:12:38.250 --> 01:12:40.170 align:middle line:84%
just want efficient
execution and to get sense

01:12:40.170 --> 01:12:41.630 align:middle line:84%
that they're not being
taken advantage of,

01:12:41.630 --> 01:12:43.047 align:middle line:84%
and that are seeing
a full market,

01:12:43.047 --> 01:12:45.410 align:middle line:84%
and they're able to decide
what they want to do.

01:12:45.410 --> 01:12:46.827 align:middle line:84%
And there's a bunch
of other guys,

01:12:46.827 --> 01:12:51.270 align:middle line:84%
but those are some of the
main market participants here.

01:12:51.270 --> 01:12:55.567 align:middle line:84%
So price making here, at least
on the dealer side, which

01:12:55.567 --> 01:12:56.650 align:middle line:90%
is what I'm familiar with.

01:12:56.650 --> 01:12:58.025 align:middle line:84%
I've never worked
at a prop shop,

01:12:58.025 --> 01:13:00.370 align:middle line:84%
so I don't know if those
guys are much different.

01:13:00.370 --> 01:13:03.190 align:middle line:84%
We start by getting a
good mid-market price,

01:13:03.190 --> 01:13:05.130 align:middle line:84%
either from liquidity
providers, if it's

01:13:05.130 --> 01:13:07.130 align:middle line:84%
something that trades
actively, or from a model,

01:13:07.130 --> 01:13:11.390 align:middle line:84%
if it's something that's
a little off the run.

01:13:11.390 --> 01:13:13.310 align:middle line:84%
You're then going to
skew the mid-market price

01:13:13.310 --> 01:13:15.477 align:middle line:84%
to what you're actually
going to offer the guy based

01:13:15.477 --> 01:13:16.830 align:middle line:90%
on a whole lot of stuff.

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:18.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe risk-- what is
your current position

01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:19.475 align:middle line:90%
on this product?

01:13:19.475 --> 01:13:20.850 align:middle line:84%
If this is a
risk-reducing trade,

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:22.030 align:middle line:84%
you're willing to be
more aggressive just

01:13:22.030 --> 01:13:23.170 align:middle line:90%
to get it off your books.

01:13:23.170 --> 01:13:24.628 align:middle line:84%
If this is a
risk-increasing trade,

01:13:24.628 --> 01:13:26.712 align:middle line:84%
you're generally going to
want to be a little more

01:13:26.712 --> 01:13:29.470 align:middle line:84%
conservative because it's
going to cost you to manage it.

01:13:29.470 --> 01:13:31.070 align:middle line:90%
Who's the client?

01:13:31.070 --> 01:13:34.550 align:middle line:84%
Is this a good client that
it's important for you

01:13:34.550 --> 01:13:37.510 align:middle line:84%
to keep good relations with,
you have other transactions

01:13:37.510 --> 01:13:39.190 align:middle line:84%
as the firm, in
which case, you're

01:13:39.190 --> 01:13:41.630 align:middle line:84%
willing to be a little
more aggressive with him.

01:13:41.630 --> 01:13:44.135 align:middle line:90%
Is this a toxic client?

01:13:44.135 --> 01:13:45.510 align:middle line:84%
That's quite an
interesting study

01:13:45.510 --> 01:13:47.910 align:middle line:84%
of how do you evaluate
client toxicity?

01:13:47.910 --> 01:13:49.770 align:middle line:84%
And the classic toxic
client is, again,

01:13:49.770 --> 01:13:52.090 align:middle line:84%
is some of fast money
guy who will put out

01:13:52.090 --> 01:13:54.690 align:middle line:84%
10 requests to 10 different
dealers for the same trade

01:13:54.690 --> 01:13:56.330 align:middle line:90%
at the same time.

01:13:56.330 --> 01:13:59.010 align:middle line:84%
So if you execute that, you'll
then find nine other guys

01:13:59.010 --> 01:14:00.030 align:middle line:90%
have executed the same.

01:14:00.030 --> 01:14:01.810 align:middle line:84%
So your hedge has
moved away from you.

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:03.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to cost
you more money to hedge,

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:05.250 align:middle line:84%
and you're probably
losing money on the trade.

01:14:05.250 --> 01:14:07.708 align:middle line:84%
But how do you determine if
that's likely to happen or not?

01:14:07.708 --> 01:14:10.090 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of market
microstructure things.

01:14:10.090 --> 01:14:11.930 align:middle line:90%
Also, volatility.

01:14:11.930 --> 01:14:14.290 align:middle line:84%
If the market is in a
particularly volatile period,

01:14:14.290 --> 01:14:15.250 align:middle line:84%
you're going to want
to be a little more

01:14:15.250 --> 01:14:17.650 align:middle line:84%
conservative in your pricing,
widen out your bid offer

01:14:17.650 --> 01:14:20.730 align:middle line:84%
because the market may move away
from you before you can hedge.

01:14:20.730 --> 01:14:23.510 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a whole
world of prediction models,

01:14:23.510 --> 01:14:26.750 align:middle line:90%
and how do you look at that?

01:14:26.750 --> 01:14:29.210 align:middle line:84%
And what's your estimate of
where the next tick is up

01:14:29.210 --> 01:14:30.630 align:middle line:90%
or down and things like that.

01:14:30.630 --> 01:14:32.838 align:middle line:84%
And there's all sorts of
market microstructure there.

01:14:32.838 --> 01:14:35.410 align:middle line:84%
That's a quite interesting
area you can do.

01:14:35.410 --> 01:14:37.690 align:middle line:84%
And generally, the
trading requires

01:14:37.690 --> 01:14:39.252 align:middle line:90%
very good integrated systems.

01:14:39.252 --> 01:14:41.710 align:middle line:84%
You need to have everything
talk and everything super fast.

01:14:41.710 --> 01:14:43.970 align:middle line:84%
You can't have--
one of the banes

01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:46.528 align:middle line:84%
of our existence at a previous
firm was the credit check.

01:14:46.528 --> 01:14:48.570 align:middle line:84%
So I'd get a request from
somebody to do a trade.

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:50.650 align:middle line:84%
I had to determine if we had
open credit lines with them.

01:14:50.650 --> 01:14:53.067 align:middle line:84%
By the time that answer came
back from our credit systems,

01:14:53.067 --> 01:14:54.310 align:middle line:90%
the deal had gone away.

01:14:54.310 --> 01:14:56.880 align:middle line:90%
So that was a bit annoying.

01:14:56.880 --> 01:14:58.630 align:middle line:84%
I think I'll end there
and just open it up

01:14:58.630 --> 01:14:59.410 align:middle line:90%
for any more questions.

01:14:59.410 --> 01:15:00.530 align:middle line:84%
I think we've got a
couple of minutes still,

01:15:00.530 --> 01:15:02.488 align:middle line:84%
so I'm happy to yack
about anything, as I said.

01:15:02.488 --> 01:15:08.510 align:middle line:84%
So otherwise, I think
we'll call it a day there.

01:15:08.510 --> 01:15:09.580 align:middle line:90%
Skip through some bonds.

01:15:09.580 --> 01:15:13.367 align:middle line:90%


01:15:13.367 --> 01:15:15.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess like one of
the main things that people have

01:15:15.950 --> 01:15:17.950 align:middle line:84%
been talking about
[INAUDIBLE] is the interest

01:15:17.950 --> 01:15:20.910 align:middle line:90%
rates in the recent quarter.

01:15:20.910 --> 01:15:25.950 align:middle line:84%
What is your opinion on the
implications that changes

01:15:25.950 --> 01:15:29.290 align:middle line:90%
in interest rates can have?

01:15:29.290 --> 01:15:32.830 align:middle line:84%
And just kind of understand
what type of considerations

01:15:32.830 --> 01:15:33.870 align:middle line:90%
are there going to be?

01:15:33.870 --> 01:15:34.470 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN:
The biggest one

01:15:34.470 --> 01:15:36.345 align:middle line:84%
from the guys actually
managing interest rate

01:15:36.345 --> 01:15:38.230 align:middle line:90%
policy is really inflation.

01:15:38.230 --> 01:15:40.110 align:middle line:84%
We've had a burst of
inflation here driven

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:41.610 align:middle line:90%
by things like the COVID funds.

01:15:41.610 --> 01:15:43.230 align:middle line:84%
We pumped an enormous
amount of money

01:15:43.230 --> 01:15:47.190 align:middle line:84%
into the economy to stop
things collapsing in COVID.

01:15:47.190 --> 01:15:49.770 align:middle line:84%
One of the ways that
showed up was inflation.

01:15:49.770 --> 01:15:52.190 align:middle line:84%
Russia invading Ukraine
also popped energy prices.

01:15:52.190 --> 01:15:54.170 align:middle line:90%
So that also popped inflation.

01:15:54.170 --> 01:15:55.530 align:middle line:90%
So that's one of the big ones.

01:15:55.530 --> 01:15:57.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, the downside is the
Fed's trying to balance

01:15:57.530 --> 01:15:58.850 align:middle line:90%
that with employment.

01:15:58.850 --> 01:16:00.393 align:middle line:84%
Generally, if rates
are high, people

01:16:00.393 --> 01:16:02.810 align:middle line:84%
are not willing to borrow money
because it costs you more.

01:16:02.810 --> 01:16:04.727 align:middle line:84%
If I'm going to start a
business or something,

01:16:04.727 --> 01:16:07.970 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to want to,
quite often, borrow money.

01:16:07.970 --> 01:16:11.650 align:middle line:84%
The other huge connected
thing in that is home prices.

01:16:11.650 --> 01:16:14.870 align:middle line:84%
When you look at in my lifetime,
what have I spent money on?

01:16:14.870 --> 01:16:17.490 align:middle line:84%
My house is overwhelmingly
the most expensive thing

01:16:17.490 --> 01:16:18.450 align:middle line:90%
I've bought.

01:16:18.450 --> 01:16:19.930 align:middle line:84%
And then also,
every time you move,

01:16:19.930 --> 01:16:22.350 align:middle line:84%
you buy furniture and paint
and a new roof and this.

01:16:22.350 --> 01:16:24.630 align:middle line:84%
So that's also a huge
driver of economic thing.

01:16:24.630 --> 01:16:26.730 align:middle line:84%
So I think in terms of
we've just, I think,

01:16:26.730 --> 01:16:30.170 align:middle line:84%
just kicked into an
interest rate cutting cycle.

01:16:30.170 --> 01:16:31.925 align:middle line:84%
I said a few weeks
ago they dropped it.

01:16:31.925 --> 01:16:34.050 align:middle line:84%
There's some arguments that
they kept the rates too

01:16:34.050 --> 01:16:35.452 align:middle line:90%
high for too long.

01:16:35.452 --> 01:16:36.910 align:middle line:84%
But I think they've
started to cut.

01:16:36.910 --> 01:16:39.243 align:middle line:84%
So I think the kind of things
that you would look to see

01:16:39.243 --> 01:16:42.130 align:middle line:84%
is things like mortgages,
home sales activity, business

01:16:42.130 --> 01:16:43.950 align:middle line:90%
startups, stuff like that.

01:16:43.950 --> 01:16:46.670 align:middle line:84%
It's quite pervasive in terms
of how much-- how expensive

01:16:46.670 --> 01:16:49.630 align:middle line:84%
is money is a huge driver
of a lot of activity.

01:16:49.630 --> 01:16:52.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I answered
your question or not, but OK.

01:16:52.950 --> 01:16:56.910 align:middle line:90%
So anything else?

01:16:56.910 --> 01:16:58.030 align:middle line:90%
Nope?

01:16:58.030 --> 01:17:03.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just one question
with the fixed income modeling.

01:17:03.510 --> 01:17:08.150 align:middle line:84%
I would guess that forecasting
or predicting rate changes

01:17:08.150 --> 01:17:12.095 align:middle line:84%
would take a real
back seat to hedging.

01:17:12.095 --> 01:17:14.220 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: Well,
again, that's a subtlety--

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:15.323 align:middle line:90%


01:17:15.323 --> 01:17:16.510 align:middle line:90%
It's a-- there's a subtley--

01:17:16.510 --> 01:17:17.790 align:middle line:90%


01:17:17.790 --> 01:17:19.170 align:middle line:90%
--a subtley difference here.

01:17:19.170 --> 01:17:21.290 align:middle line:84%
There's two kinds of
fixed income research.

01:17:21.290 --> 01:17:23.910 align:middle line:84%
There is the economics
client-facing research, which

01:17:23.910 --> 01:17:25.628 align:middle line:90%
is a lot about that stuff.

01:17:25.628 --> 01:17:27.170 align:middle line:84%
Here's my prediction
for the economy.

01:17:27.170 --> 01:17:29.670 align:middle line:84%
Here's if the rates cut, this
is what I think you should do.

01:17:29.670 --> 01:17:30.650 align:middle line:90%
Here's the positions.

01:17:30.650 --> 01:17:32.390 align:middle line:84%
And then there's the
internal area, which is I am,

01:17:32.390 --> 01:17:35.150 align:middle line:84%
which is more about pricing and
hedging of transactions, which

01:17:35.150 --> 01:17:36.530 align:middle line:90%
we don't make any predictions.

01:17:36.530 --> 01:17:37.170 align:middle line:90%
We don't care.

01:17:37.170 --> 01:17:38.712 align:middle line:84%
We just want to know
how to hedge it.

01:17:38.712 --> 01:17:40.090 align:middle line:90%
And at that point, we're good.

01:17:40.090 --> 01:17:42.400 align:middle line:84%
So they're quite
different in outlook,

01:17:42.400 --> 01:17:45.162 align:middle line:84%
even though they're both
fixed income research.

01:17:45.162 --> 01:17:47.120 align:middle line:84%
And I have no opinion on
where rates are going.

01:17:47.120 --> 01:17:48.880 align:middle line:84%
If I did, I wouldn't be
standing in front of you here.

01:17:48.880 --> 01:17:51.422 align:middle line:84%
I'd be sitting in my Caribbean
island, with the piña coladas.

01:17:51.422 --> 01:17:52.780 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

01:17:52.780 --> 01:17:56.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could you also
comment on the municipal bonds,

01:17:56.000 --> 01:17:59.502 align:middle line:84%
what the analytical
challenges are there?

01:17:59.502 --> 01:18:01.960 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN: So a couple
of the challenges there are,

01:18:01.960 --> 01:18:05.760 align:middle line:84%
again, there's a set of bonds
that are very idiosyncratic,

01:18:05.760 --> 01:18:06.840 align:middle line:90%
maybe the project bonds.

01:18:06.840 --> 01:18:11.380 align:middle line:84%
So this is a bond where the
revenues come off of, say, tolls

01:18:11.380 --> 01:18:12.680 align:middle line:90%
on the Pennsylvania Turnpike.

01:18:12.680 --> 01:18:14.802 align:middle line:84%
They'll issue bonds
supported by that.

01:18:14.802 --> 01:18:15.760 align:middle line:90%
I don't know about you.

01:18:15.760 --> 01:18:18.220 align:middle line:84%
I have no idea on the traffic
in the Pennsylvania Turnpike

01:18:18.220 --> 01:18:18.960 align:middle line:90%
and what that's like.

01:18:18.960 --> 01:18:20.500 align:middle line:84%
Could it be-- so a
lot of these things

01:18:20.500 --> 01:18:22.420 align:middle line:84%
got a big whack in COVID,
for example, because nobody

01:18:22.420 --> 01:18:23.532 align:middle line:90%
went anywhere for a year.

01:18:23.532 --> 01:18:25.240 align:middle line:84%
So there was no revenues
to support them.

01:18:25.240 --> 01:18:26.660 align:middle line:90%
So they all got [INAUDIBLE].

01:18:26.660 --> 01:18:29.940 align:middle line:84%
A lot of other munis,
it's about what do

01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:31.560 align:middle line:90%
you think future tax policy is?

01:18:31.560 --> 01:18:33.520 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose the
average federal rate is,

01:18:33.520 --> 01:18:34.920 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, 30% right now.

01:18:34.920 --> 01:18:37.780 align:middle line:84%
So that means I'm willing to
take 70% of the Treasury rate

01:18:37.780 --> 01:18:39.260 align:middle line:90%
for a muni bond.

01:18:39.260 --> 01:18:42.140 align:middle line:84%
When I look forward, do I think
tax rates are going up or down?

01:18:42.140 --> 01:18:43.640 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea.

01:18:43.640 --> 01:18:46.040 align:middle line:84%
I would suspect up, but
you never know just given

01:18:46.040 --> 01:18:47.260 align:middle line:90%
the deficit situation.

01:18:47.260 --> 01:18:48.240 align:middle line:90%
But who knows?

01:18:48.240 --> 01:18:51.045 align:middle line:90%
So there's that kind of issue.

01:18:51.045 --> 01:18:52.920 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the other issues
more on the trading

01:18:52.920 --> 01:18:56.320 align:middle line:84%
and the quant side is, how do
you figure out who's got them?

01:18:56.320 --> 01:18:57.760 align:middle line:90%
So I want--

01:18:57.760 --> 01:19:00.940 align:middle line:84%
I have somebody who wants to buy
a bunch of AA New Jersey bonds.

01:19:00.940 --> 01:19:02.280 align:middle line:90%
How do I source those?

01:19:02.280 --> 01:19:03.660 align:middle line:90%
So that's a lot of data.

01:19:03.660 --> 01:19:05.520 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the
reasons munis are still

01:19:05.520 --> 01:19:08.360 align:middle line:84%
very voice-driven is the sales
guys know where the bonds are,

01:19:08.360 --> 01:19:09.935 align:middle line:84%
so they can get
them out for you.

01:19:09.935 --> 01:19:11.560 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, the machines
just have no idea

01:19:11.560 --> 01:19:12.810 align:middle line:90%
who's got the bonds right now.

01:19:12.810 --> 01:19:15.360 align:middle line:84%
So it's hard to source
the product at that point.

01:19:15.360 --> 01:19:17.540 align:middle line:84%
So that's some of
the complexities.

01:19:17.540 --> 01:19:20.822 align:middle line:90%


01:19:20.822 --> 01:19:22.280 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER: Well,
thank you very much.

01:19:22.280 --> 01:19:23.905 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW GUNSTENSEN:
Thank you very much.

01:19:23.905 --> 01:19:26.370 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

01:19:26.370 --> 01:19:39.000 align:middle line:90%